\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Candidates duck inflation issue on eve of Argentina election\b0\par\par\uc2 961 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 It is on the lips of the people, but not so much on the lips of those who want to govern them. Inflation, which daily eats away at Argentines' wages and adds to the ranks of the poor, is a topic that the country's leading presidential candidates have sought to dodge ten days before the primary elections.\par \par \uc2 Deeper discussions on the problem, which would require unpopular measures, have been left for later on the candidates' agenda. Instead, they have chosen to prioritize possibly more fruitful agendas in terms of votes, such as public safety and job creation.\par \par \uc2 On the streets, however, rising prices are the number one cause of angst cited by voters of any political preference or region, different surveys indicate. A July survey by the University of San Andr\u233\'00\'E9s found that 55% of Argentines say inflation is Argentina's biggest current obstacle. Insecurity comes in second, mentioned by 38% of the population.\par \par \uc2 "Before we could have a barbecue on the weekends, now the money isn't enough," complains Miguel Garc\u237\'00\'EDa, 56, a janitor outside his building in an affluent Buenos Aires neighborhood. And what do the candidates intend to do about it? He shrugs: "I don't know, they just promise and never deliver."\par \par \uc2 On the Peronist side, Sergio Massa has avoided the topic as much as possible, since he has been economy minister for more than a year and has so far failed to halt the devaluation of the peso. On the opposition right, Buenos Aires Mayor Horacio Larreta and former Security Minister Patricia Bullrich - who are vying for a seat in the first round - repeat the mantra of lowering spending, but do not detail how.\par \par \uc2 "Neither of the two largest coalitions is able to address the issue as an advantage, because both doubled inflation in their governments," says Artemio L\u243\'00\'F3pez, director of consultancy Equis, referring to the administrations of Republican \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  (2015-2019) and the current president, Peronist \uc2 Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The exception is the ultra-liberal Javier Milei, who uses the country's economic malaise as his main political springboard, but does not expose concrete plans on his proposal to dollarize Argentina, according to analysts. "It's the issue that Milei can lean on the most, but he can't pin down a government project, he proposes \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on specific issues," says L\u243\'00\'F3pez.\par \par \uc2 The primaries in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 , called Paso (Primary, Open, Simultaneous and Compulsory), are usually seen as a major national poll for the first round on October 22. This time, however, they could also change the course of the election, depending on who wins in the opposition coalition Juntos por el Cambio. Larreta is more center-leaning, and Bullrich is more to the right.\par \par \uc2 So far, the scenario is one of great uncertainty, with polls showing a close race between the two, who register something close to 15% of voting intentions. Together, they account for about a third of voters, while the Peronist coalition Union for the Homeland is close behind. Milei has lost steam in the last month and registers figures ranging from 12% to 20%, depending on the survey.\par \par \uc2 There is also a significant portion of the population that has not decided on a candidate, such as Miguel, a janitor, or intends to vote blank or null. The feeling of "I'm fed up" and disillusionment with politics is a hallmark of this election, after successive governments failed to contain the escalation of inflation and avoid the country's third major economic crisis in 40 years of democracy.\par \par \uc2 Prices have risen 116% in the last 12 months, considering the accumulated index until June - the second highest figure in Latin America, behind Venezuela. Some of the causes are an insistent deficit, high foreign debt, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  without credibility and a lack of dollars in public coffers. Four out of ten Argentines are below the poverty line.\par \par \uc2 "The solutions include eliminating subsidies, cutting public services and other measures that are unpalatable in an election campaign, which is why the candidates shy away, even though they all have studies on the path they intend to take," says economist Juan Telechea, director of the ITE (Institute of Labor and Economics of the Germ\u225\'00\'E1n Abdala Foundation).\par \par \uc2 Peronist minister Sergio Massa, for example, has focused his speeches on national industry, and always sides with workers: "Two models of country are at stake, one that bets on national production, genuine employment and the sale of Argentine labor to the world, and another that seeks only financial speculation," he declared at a rally this week.\par \par \uc2 Horacio Larreta, meanwhile, is banking on his experience running the capital Buenos Aires, saying "we're going to do it because we've done it before". To the sound of hopeful or heroic songs, he is one of those who lists the most economic proposals, talking about spending control, tax cuts and boosting exports, but without going into detail.\par \par \uc2 He also advocates more conservative measures in the area of security, as does Patricia Bullrich. Macri's former minister talks about "putting order" in the country and encourages polarization with Peronism. On finance, she also advocates ending the limit on the purchase of dollars and an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but has recently been the target of jokes for statements in which she showed ignorance of the area.\par \par \uc2 Milei's campaign, on the other hand, stands out from the lukewarm tone of the other candidates. To the sound of rock music, wearing a leather jacket and always surrounded by a crowd of young people, the libertarian talks about ending the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and radically cleaning up the state against corruption. His slogan, always in bold letters, is a little more direct: "Long live freedom".\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230803ej8300elz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
