\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Effects of the "yuanization" of the  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2 : between loans to import and ill-informed  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 852 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 As the days of July went by, certain discrepancies began to be noticed between what the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported to the press about its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and what was later confirmed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself.\par \par \uc2 Doubts were raised in the market, but it was not known for sure what was really happening, because the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  misreported its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and mainly with favorable numbers for the entity in the press releases.\par \par \uc2 From here we will try to give a description of what is happening and how to analyze the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's future press releases. The origin of the problem\par \par \uc2 On June 28 we witnessed the first major deviation from what was to be a confusing data collection that generated uncertainty as to how the exchange rate scenario was turning out for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and what scenario was taking place.\par \par \uc2 From that moment on, the errors started to be not only more frequent, but there were also -accumulating for the time being-, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  underestimates for u$s835 million.\par \par \uc2 Why is this difference? It basically arises from an unannounced change in the way of communicating the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, since previously the data was reported to the press in a unified manner. That is to say, whether dollars or yuans were sold, the data was netted and condensed into a dollar amount.\par \par \uc2 But the gradual "yuanization" of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market led the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  mainly in two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dollars and yuans. So far, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has used yuan for about u$s3,600 million (between exchange \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and payment to the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 ), of which it has approved for free availability u$s5,000 million, and another tranche authorized for use (which is not the same as conversion) for another u$s5,000 million. In addition, the Chinese swap is for US$ 18 billion.\par \par \uc2 What do these numbers mean? That since May so far, it has been used for: 72% of authorized amount of free availability. 36% of the amount authorized so far. 20% of the total amount of the swap.\par \par \uc2 So far in July alone, yuans have been sold for u$s910 million in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while, if only the dollar figure is analyzed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a seller of u$s47 million. Such a huge difference denotes a clear will of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to release yuans (at the cost of assuming debt with the Asian country) instead of getting rid of the so precious dollars.\par \par \uc2 Therefore, in order to analyze in correct terms the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  must make in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to meet the demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not only the dollar figure should be included, but now also the yuan figure.\par \par \uc2 There you will find the answer to how much it is costing the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain an exchange rate like the current one, and at the same time, a brief look at how the yuan is playing an interesting role in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Yuanization of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market\par \par \uc2 We are witnessing a displacement of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out in dollars, to be replaced by yuans. Why? Precisely because dollars are in short supply -and in short supply-. Net reserves are around u$s7,300 million negative, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not stop getting rid of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  every day.\par \par \uc2 Here comes the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  to offer a "rescue", logically under its own interests, offering a foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loan whose terms are located in a thick fog of sub-information, but which in any case allows giving some temporary relief to the current situation at the cost of worsening it in the future, or at least contracting a loan whose repayment we do not know if it will be economically feasible or convenient.\par \par \uc2 Therefore, although the dollar scenario in July is not exaggeratedly bad, it is bad if analyzed in net terms, adding the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In only 10 rounds of July, a net u$s950 million have been sold, a few dollars away from surpassing the maximum sold in the seventh month of each year with the cap.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, imports from China are implicitly encouraged through these \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , knowing that, for the time being, the allocation of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at least somewhat more flexible than if it were in dollars. Let us recall that this year's trade deficit with the Asian country (in goods) accumulated up to May was u$s 3,150 million.\par \par \uc2 In short, China would be financing Argentina's exports, or Argentina would be requesting a loan from China to be able to import from there. But like any loan, such obligation will mature and Argentina will have to pay. How? Probably with dollars .\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, not only the sales of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are underestimated here , but also the consequences of financing them.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230717ej7h00c3v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
