\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 How high will the dollar and inflation be at the end of the year, according to the report most consulted by the private sector?\b0\par\par\uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The projections of the regional consulting firm Latin Fcus assure that Argentina will have the worst performance in the region in 2023. According to this month's Latin Focus Consensus Forecast report, one of the most consulted and used by the private sector to make projections and measure what will happen with the variables of the Argentine economy -prepared on the forecasts of more than 50 economic consulting firms and local and foreign banks-, inflation will stand at 108.4% average by the end of the year and the official dollar will reach $391 in 2023 and $791 in 2024.\par \par \uc2 "After a deterioration in the economy driven by falling domestic demand in the fourth quarter of 2022, the economy likely remained stagnant in the first quarter. Inflation increased while the peso continued to depreciate markedly. In addition, consumer confidence remained deeply pessimistic," describes the report prepared by the consulting firm.\par \par \uc2 Inflation will average 108.4% by the end of the year and the official dollar will reach $391 in 2023 and $791 in 2024.\par \par \uc2 And then it lists a series of factors that further worsened the economic situation: "exports fell, interest rates soared and there was a severe drought. Moving to the second quarter, two additional rate hikes in April and still depressed consumer confidence, along with a declining peso, represent problems for activity."\par \par \uc2 They also noted that the country's reserves are at their lowest level in nearly seven years due to drought-stricken agricultural exports and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have the worst performance in the region this year. GDP will fall amid galloping inflation, a free-falling peso, shrinking savings and sky-high interest rates. Global headwinds and drought will worsen the situation. Add to that the uncertainty in the run-up to the October general elections. Extremely high debt payment risks further cloud the outlook," the report warned.\par \par \uc2 Regarding the inflation rate, Latin Focus consultants highlighted that it is the highest since their records began. "Inflation rose to 108.8% in April, following 104.3% in March, which marked the highest inflation rate since our current records began. Average inflation is projected to be notably higher this year than in 2022 amid the depreciation of the peso and monetary financing of the fiscal deficit," they explained.\par \par \uc2 FocusEconomics panelists note two key risks: volatile energy prices and fiscal spending ahead of the October elections. As a result, they estimated that consumer prices will rise 108.4% on average in 2023, up 5.6 percentage points from the previous projection, published a month ago.\par \par \uc2 "After the strong upside surprise in April, we revised our central scenario for the future. We now assume monthly headline inflation of 8.1% per month on average through the end of the year. And we assume a correction in the official exchange rate by the end of 2023 when a new administration takes office in December," anticipated Lucila Barbeito and Diego Pereira, analysts at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At the time of the report's closing, the Argentine peso was trading at $229 per dollar, a 6.7% monthly depreciation. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sticks to a managed floating exchange rate for the peso. Meanwhile, the parallel market dollar was quoted at $472 per USD. The peso should lose more ground amid prolonged monetary financing of the fiscal deficit," FocusEconomics panelists noted. All in all, they see the official dollar ending 2023 at $391 and by the end of 2024 it will reach 791 pesos.\par \par \uc2 Finally, the consultants anticipated that Argentina's GDP will contract by 2.4% during 2023, which represents a reduction of 0.5 percentage points compared to the previous month, and will expand by only 0.4% in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Importing to control inflation: the Central Market's doubts and the Government's strategy\par \par \uc2 Debt: the Government will raise the rate of its bonds and will seek financing from the market with inflation-linked securities.\par \par \uc2 Knowledge industry: the sector warns about a growing "Argentine talent drain" to Uruguay\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230517ej5h00d9q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
