\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  plans new rate hike, more  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  as inflation soars\b0\par\par\uc2 280 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2023\par 17:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail on interest rate hike, paragraph 3)\par \par \uc2 By Jorge Otaola and Maximilian Heath\par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, May 14 (Reuters) - Argentina's government announced a package of measures to rein in soaring inflation and support the wobbly peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sunday, including rate adjustments, more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market and expedited deals with creditors.\par \par \uc2 The measures include an interest rate hike by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the economy ministry said in a statement. The ministry did not elaborate, but an official source told Reuters the hike would be 600 basis points, bringing the rate up to 97%.\par \par \uc2 The rate hike will take effect Monday, the source added.\par \par \uc2 The South American nation is battling to bring down inflation that hit 109% on an annual basis in April. It also faces tumbling confidence in the peso and dwindling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves that are threatening the government's finances.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also increase its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and double down on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation plan, the ministry said.\par \par \uc2 An agreement with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  to dole out funds to the cash-strapped nation will be sped up as well, the ministry added.\par \par \uc2 More measures are set to be announced in coming days, according to the ministry.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Alberto Fernandez\uc2 's government is looking to bring the economic situation under control as elections near, with opinion polls showing flagging support for the ruling Peronist party. (Reporting by Jorge Otaola and Maximilian Heath in Buenos Airies Writing by Adam Jourdan and Kylie Madry Editing by Matthew Lewis and Grant McCool) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-5-14T16:02:20.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230514ej5e00nrl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
