\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 U.S. would support \uc2 IMF\uc2  to speed up loan delivery to Argentina\b0\par\par\uc2 Eric Mart\u237\'00\'EDn \par \uc2 653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 May 12 (Bloomberg) -- The administration of U.S. President Joseph R. Biden would support accelerating disbursements under the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's $44 billion program for Argentina if the nation's authorities can successfully negotiate a new schedule with the international lender, according to U.S. officials familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Under the current program, Argentina will receive $10.6 billion from the \uc2 IMF\uc2  between June and December. However, the South American nation, pressured by dwindling international reserves, out-of-control inflation and a record agricultural drought, is seeking to advance some or all of that \uc2 IMF\uc2  financing to June.\par \par \uc2 The United States would support frontloading the resources because it sees the need to prevent Argentina's economic crisis from worsening, said the officials, who asked not to be identified without permission to speak publicly on the matter.\par \par \uc2 The Argentine government plans to argue to the \uc2 IMF\uc2  that the severe drought the nation is suffering is beyond its control, and it needs the funds as soon as possible to control the crisis.\par \par \uc2 U.S. support is important, given its status as the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's largest shareholder and the fund's desire to make decisions by consensus.\par \par \uc2 But negotiating an early distribution of the loan with \uc2 IMF\uc2  staff will not be an easy task for Argentina. The fund is likely to ask for conditions that could be difficult for the country to meet, and Argentina has a long history of failing to meet targets set by the \uc2 IMF\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another U.S. official cautioned that the United States would have to review any staffing arrangement and consult with \uc2 IMF\uc2  leaders and other board members before taking an official position.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2 , the National Security Council and Argentina's Economy Ministry declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's bond prices have fallen sharply three years after the South American nation restructured $65 billion of its foreign debt.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 IMF\uc2  spokeswoman said the fund continues to work very closely with the Argentine authorities to strengthen the country's program in the context of the drought, and that discussions on the next review are ongoing and continuing in a constructive manner.\par \par \uc2 Argentina is the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's largest borrower and has a complicated history with the fund after 22 different programs over nearly 70 years did little to fix a crisis-prone economy.\par \par \uc2 The current program is already off track with the government likely to miss key targets through the end of March, while annual inflation hit 104% and the peso plunged on parallel markets. Those setbacks forced the authorities to go back to the drawing board to review the loan.\par \par \uc2 Argentina also faces a volatile presidential election this year and accepting a looser financial program could be seen by the opposition as a favor to the government ahead of the October vote.\par \par \uc2 Complicated history\par \par \uc2 Although unusual by its standards, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  has advanced cash to Argentina before. In 2018, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  approved advancing disbursements to the government of former President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  as the economy plummeted into a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis triggered by another significant drought. However, more cash upfront did not stabilize the economy and the program was suspended around the 2019 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 To complicate matters further, Argentina's Vice President \uc2 Cristina Fernandez de Kirchner\uc2 , who over the years has repeatedly criticized the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , last week blamed the existing program for the country's economic malaise, complaining that the fund's rules prevented the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the peso's slide.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy is facing one of its most severe moments with drought sending activity into recession and international reserves falling even after the Fund disbursed $5.4 billion in new funds in early April, bringing disbursements under the Fund's expanded facility to $28.9 billion.\par \par \uc2 --with assistance from Patrick Gillespie and Scott Squires.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230512ej5c00b7o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
