\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 U.S. backs accelerating \uc2 IMF\uc2  disbursements to drought-stricken Argentina\b0\par\par\uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The Biden Administration would support accelerating disbursements under Argentina's US$44 billion program with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) if the country's authorities can successfully negotiate a new schedule with the institution, according to U.S. officials familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Under the current program, Argentina is due to receive US$ 10.6 billion from the \uc2 IMF\uc2  between June and December. However, the South American nation, pressured by falling reserves, out-of-control inflation and an unprecedented drought, is seeking to advance some or all of the \uc2 IMF\uc2  financing to June.\par \par \uc2 The United States would favor advancing payments because it sees the need to prevent Argentina's economic crisis from worsening, said the officials, who asked not to be identified without authorization to speak publicly.\par \par \uc2 The Argentine government plans to argue to the \uc2 IMF\uc2  that the country's severe drought is beyond its control and that it needs the funds as soon as possible to stem the crisis.\par \par \uc2 U.S. support is important, given its status as the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's largest shareholder and the multilateral organization's desire to make decisions by consensus.\par \par \uc2 But negotiating early loan disbursement with \uc2 IMF\uc2  staff will not be easy for Argentina. The Fund is likely to demand conditions that could be difficult for the country to meet, and Argentina has a long history of failing to meet targets set for it by the \uc2 IMF\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another U.S. official cautioned that the United States would have to review any staff agreement and consult with \uc2 IMF\uc2  management and other board members before taking an official position.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2 , the National Security Council and Argentina's Economy Ministry declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 IMF\uc2  spokeswoman said the Fund continues to work very closely with the Argentine authorities to strengthen the country's program in the context of the drought, and that discussions on the next review are ongoing and continuing in a constructive manner.\par \par \uc2 Argentina is the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's largest borrower and has a complicated history with the Fund after 22 different programs over nearly 70 years did little to fix a crisis-prone economy.\par \par \uc2 The current program is already off track, and the government is likely to have missed key targets by the end of March, while annual inflation reached 104% and the peso plummeted on parallel markets. These setbacks forced the authorities to go back to the drawing board to review the loan.\par \par \uc2 In addition, Argentina faces an unstable presidential election this year, and the opposition may consider that accepting a more flexible financial program favors the government in the run-up to the October elections.\par \par \uc2 Although unusual by its standards, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  has advanced funds to Argentina before. In 2018, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  approved advancing disbursements to the government of former President \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  as the economy plunged into a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis triggered by another major drought. However, the advance of funds did not stabilize the economy and the program was suspended around the 2019 presidential elections.\par \par \uc2 Further complicating matters, Argentina's Vice President \uc2 Cristina Fernandez de Kirchner\uc2 , who has repeatedly criticized the \uc2 IMF\uc2  over the years, last week blamed the existing program for the country's economic woes, complaining that the fund's rules prevented the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the peso's slide.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy is facing one of its most severe moments, with a drought that has pushed activity into recession and international reserves plummeting even after the Fund disbursed US$5.4 billion in fresh financing in early April, bringing disbursements under the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's extended facility to US$28.9 billion.\par \par \uc2 Unlimited benefits with your Gesti\u243\'00\'F3n subscription! Enjoy up to 70% discount on more than 300 Subscribers' Club promotions, which you can also use as many times as you want. Get to know them here. If you are not a subscriber yet, purchase your plan here.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230504ej54009ro\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw109\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1635\pichgoal600
