penses, that are currently presented in nominal terms, restated at year-end \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The criteria followed in prior years was to present them in real terms, i.e., net of the effect of inflation on the assets and liabilities that generated these income or expenses. The figures for the year ended December 31, 2021 and 2020 presented in this annual report have been modified for comparability purposes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Income Tax Expense\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Income tax expense includes current and deferred taxes. Current income tax is measured as the amount expected to be paid (or recovered, to the extent applicable) to tax authorities based on the taxable profit for the period. Deferred taxes includes the effect of temporary differences originating in the different basis for measuring assets and liabilities according to accounting and tax criteria and of the existing net losses and unused tax credits susceptible of deduction of future taxable income computed by considering the tax rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table of Contents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Results of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 In the following discussion, references to increases or decreases in any period are made by comparison with the prior period, except as the context otherwise indicates. For a reconciliation of the operating results of our operating segments for the periods indicated to our consolidated results of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , see "Note 31 to our audited consolidated financial statements included elsewhere in this annual report".\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Due to the transaction described in "Item 4.A - History and Development of the Company", the company classified the results associated with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Yguaz\u250\'00\'FA Cementos S.A. as a discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which represented the entire cement operating segment in Paraguay until August 21, 2020. With such results classified as discontinued transactions, the cement segment in Paraguay is no longer presented in the segment note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Year Ended December 31, 2022, compared to the Year Ended December 31, 2021\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth our statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income for 2022 and 2021:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the Year EndedDecember 31,                Variation \par 2022                                          2021          Amount       (%) \par (in millions of Ps., except percentages) \par Revenue                                       145,132.6     143,500.5    1,632.1      1.1 \par Cost of sales                                 (105,939.9)   (98,144.8)   (7,795.1)    7.9 \par Gross profit                                  39,192.7      45,355.7     (6,163.0)    (13.6) \par Selling and administrative expenses           (12,510.6)    (12,327.9)   (182.7)      1.5 \par Impairment of property, plant and equipment   -             (297.7)      297.7        (100.0) \par Other gains and losses                        3,385.4       408.0        2,977.4      729.8 \par Tax on debits and credits to bank accounts    (1,455.2)     (1,446.2)    (9.0)        0.6 \par Finance results, net \par Exchange rate differences                     (7,419.1)     (3,207.7)    (4,211.4)    131.3 \par Gain on net monetary position                 13,747.2      3,911.8      9,835.4      251.4 \par Financial income                              1,626.3       1,991.0      (364.7)      (18.3) \par Financial expenses                            (25,563.7)    (2,612.4)    (22,951.3)   878.6 \par Profit before taxes                           11,003.0      31,774.6     (20,771.6)   (65.4) \par Income tax expense \par Current                                       (4,104.7)     (12,931.4)   8,826.7      (68.3) \par Deferred                                      (5,091.4)     (6,485.2)    1,393.8      (21.5) \par Net profit                                    1,806.9       12,358.0     (10,551.1)   (85.4) \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ________________\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenues\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our revenues increased Ps. 1,632 million, or 1.1%, from Ps. 143,501 million in 2021 to Ps. 145,133 million in 2022, mainly due to the sales volume increase of 9.7%, 11.0%, 48.1% and 4.8% in our cement, masonry cement and lime segment, concrete segment, aggregates segment and railroad segment, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 Cement, masonry cement and lime segment : Revenues from our cement, masonry cement and lime segment, without considering the eliminations between segments, decreased Ps. 175 million, from Ps. 128,418 million in 2021 to Ps. 128,244 million in 2022, mainly due to an average sales price decrease of 8.9%, partially offset by an increase of 9.7% in sales volume.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 Concrete segment: Revenues from our concrete segment increased Ps. 1,894 million, from Ps. 10,372 million in 2021 to Ps. 12,266 million in 2022, mainly due to an increase of 11.0% in sales volume and an average sales price increase of 6.6%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table of Contents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 Railroad segment : Revenues from our railroad segment, without considering the eliminations between segments, increased Ps. 36 million, from Ps. 11,648 million in 2021 to Ps. 11,684 million in 2022, mainly due to an increase of 4.8% in sales volume, driven by strong demand for building materials, offset by a decline of 4.3% in the average sales price.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 Aggregates segment : Revenues from our aggregates segment, without considering the eliminations between segments, increased Ps. 1,459 million, from Ps. 2,158 million in 2021 to Ps. 3,617 million in 2022, mainly due to an increase of 48.1% in sales volume and an average price increase of 13.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 Others segment : Revenues from Recycomb S.A.U., without considering the eliminations between segments, increased Ps. 45 million, from Ps. 867 million in 2021 to Ps. 911 million in 2022.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cost of sales\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our cost of sales increased Ps. 7,795 million, or 7.9%, from Ps. 98,145 million for 2021 to Ps. 105,940 million for 2022, mostly as a consequence of the higher sales volume. The main contributors to our cost of sales increase during the period were (1) Ps. 3,045 million in thermal and electrical energy costs, due to higher sales volume and higher unitary costs. In 2022, thermal and electrical energy incidence in our unitary cost in U.S. dollars increased by 45% and 12% respectively; (2) Ps. 1,160 million in higher freight costs, principally due to higher sales volume and inflation (in 2022, incidence of freights in our unitary cost in U.S. dollars increased by 25%); and (3) a Ps. 1,414 million increase in depreciation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table sets forth the reconciliation of our production costs to our cost of sales for the years indicated:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of and for the YearEnded December 31, \par 2022                                             2021 \par (in millions of Ps.) \par Purchases and production expenses for the year   109,774.8   98,607.2 \par (+) Inventories at the beginning of the year     22,949.2    22,486.8 \par (-) Inventories at the end of the year           26,784.1    22,949.2 \par Cost of sales                                    105,939.9   98,144.8 \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The cost of sales of our segments is set forth below, eliminations between segments are not considered:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 Cement, masonry cement and lime segment: Cost of sales from our cement, masonry cement and lime segment, without considering the eliminations between segments, increased Ps. 6,525 million, or 8.0%, from Ps. 81,583 million in 2021 to Ps. 88,108 million in 2022. This increase in cost of sales was mainly due to (1) higher thermal and electrical energy costs as a consequence of increased sales volume and higher unitary costs in US dollars; (2) an increase in depreciation due to the completion of L'Amal\u237\'00\'ED's expansion project; (3) higher freight costs mainly due to increased outbound and inbound transportation needs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 Concrete segment : Cost of sales from our concrete segment, without considering the eliminations between segments, increased Ps. 1,148 million, or 10.3%, from Ps. 11,111 million in 2021 to Ps. 12,259 million in 2022, mainly reflecting an increase in costs due to higher sales volume and higher costs related to salaries, wages and social security charges.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 Railroad segment : Cost of sales from our railroad segment increased Ps. 730 million, or 5.8%, from Ps. 12,525 million in 2021 to Ps. 13,255 million in 2022, mainly impacted by higher transported volume coupled with higher depreciation, maintenance, contractors and fuel costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 Aggregates segment: Cost of sales from our aggregates segment increased Ps. 1,018 million, or 44.2%, from Ps. 2,303 million in 2021 to Ps. 3,322 million in 2022, mainly reflecting an increase in costs due to higher sales volume, higher equipment leases and an increase in salaries, wages and social security charges.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8226\'20\'22 Others segment: Cost of sales from Recycomb S.A.U. segment increased Ps. 1 million, or 0.2%, from Ps. 585 million in 2021 to Ps. 586 million in 2022.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table of Contents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gross profit\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Due to the factors mentioned above, our gross profit decreased Ps. 6,163 million, or 13.6%, from Ps. 45,356 million in 2021 to Ps. 39,193 million in 2022. Our gross margin (gross profit divided by revenues and expressed as a percentage) was contracted by 460 basis points, from 31.6% in 2021 to 27.0% in 2022.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Selling and administrative expenses\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our selling and administrative expenses increased Ps. 183 million, or 1.5%, from Ps. 12,328 million in 2021 to Ps. 12,511 million in 2022, mainly due to (1) an increase in salaries, wages and social security charges; (2) an increase in transport and travel expenses; and (3) an increase in depreciation. However, this was partially offset by the recognition of an allowance for doubtful receivables in the railroad segment in 2021, which affected the comparison.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other gains and losses\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our other gains and losses increased Ps. 2,977 million, or 729.8%, from Ps. 408 million in 2021 to Ps. 3,385 million in 2022, mainly due to the sale of a non-strategic property in Olavarr\u237\'00\'EDa.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tax on bank accounts debits and credits\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our tax on bank accounts debits and credits increased Ps. 9 million, or 0.6%, from Ps. 1,446 million in 2021 to Ps. 1,455 million in 2022, related to the volume of monetary transactions carried out the respective fiscal year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial results, net\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our financial results decreased Ps. 17,692 million, from a gain of Ps. 83 million in 2021 to a loss of Ps. 17,609 million in 2022, principally due to (1) a loss of Ps. 17,636 million from securities transactions; (2) an increase in gain on net monetary position of Ps. 9,835 million; (3) a higher loss of Ps. 4,211 million from exchange rate differences; and (4) a lower net financial income and expense of Ps. 5,680 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our financial expenses increased Ps. 22,951 million, or 878.6%, from Ps. 2,612 million in 2021 to Ps. 25,564 million in 2022, mainly due to a loss of Ps. 17,636 million from securities transactions of and an increase in interest expenses of Ps. 4,744 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our financial income decreased Ps. 365 million, or 18.3%, from Ps. 1,991 million for 2021 to Ps. 1,626 million for 2022.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Income tax expense\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our income tax expense decreased Ps. 10,220 million, or 52,6%, from Ps. 19,417 million in 2021 to Ps. 9,196 million in 2022. The effective tax rate was 83.6% in 2022 compared to 61.1% in 2021.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table of Contents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table presents our effective tax rate reconciliation for each year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the year endedDecember 31, \par 2022                                                                  2021 \par (amounts in millions of Ps.) \par Profit before income tax expense                                      11,003.0    31,774.5 \par Statutory rate                                                        35          %   35   % \par Income tax at statutory rate                                          (3,851.1)   (11,121.1) \par Adjustments for calculation of the effective income tax: \par Valuation allowance of specific tax loss carryforwards                (4,490.2)   - \par Recovery of tax losses / Unrecognized tax loss                        5.5         (462.0) \par Effects of the inflation adjustment for accounting and tax purposes   (825.9)     (330.7) \par Change in tax rate                                                    0.9         (7,514.9) \par Other non-taxable income or non-deductible expense net                (35.4)      12.0 \par Income tax expense                                                    (9,196.2)   (19,416.7) \par Income tax expense \par Current                                                               (4,104.7)   (12,931.4) \par Deferred                                                              (5,091.4)   (6,485.2) \par Total                                                                 (9,196.1)   (19,416.6) \par 