\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 La Repregunta. Axel Kaiser: "If Argentina had free trade, 70% of the caste would disappear immediately".\b0\par\par\uc2 Luciana V\u225\'00\'E1zquez \par \uc2 6,966 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 "You destroy democracy by annihilating economic freedom: people don't want to live under a system that condemns them to misery and prevents them from getting ahead," he says. "Democracy has to be liberal. Otherwise, it has no advantage over any dictatorship of the worst kind," he argues. "There are \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  models that are more responsible than others and it has a lot to do with the idiosyncrasy of each country", he analyzes and develops: "Argentina cannot have an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it will never be allowed". "The Chilean Constitution forbids the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy public debt. It is totally forbidden to finance the government. In the new Constitution, they wanted to eliminate that restriction," he details. "In Chile, an example that is not as dramatic as Argentina's, in the last few years, there were 80 billion dollars of capital flight. Why? Because we have started to follow a path more similar to Argentina's," he is alarmed.\par \par \uc2  "In Latin America, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is used politically to finance public spending", he says and adds: "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is used by the mafia that governs our countries and ends up destroying the quality of life of the people". "The Argentine political class and elites are rapacious," he questions. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very tempting for politicians", he continues.\par \par \uc2 "There is a consensus among libertarians that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be eliminated," he concludes.\par \par \uc2 Argentina is a parasitic society where all interest groups try to live at the expense of everyone else," he defines. "The political caste are those who have decision-making power and who govern for their own interest and the interest of those who support them", he specifies. "Milei is the only one who denounces the great problem of Argentina as it has to be", he believes.\par \par \uc2 "It is a fallacy to say, as Eva Per\u243\'00\'F3n used to say, that where a need is born, a right is born: needs are not rights", he challenges.\par \par \uc2 Poverty is overcome with economic growth, not with distribution", he believes. "In Argentina, the State is the cause of people being in poverty," he criticizes. "There may be limited and focused aid policies, but they do not imply the conception that every need is a right," he clarifies. "The notion of needs is extremely broad and endless," he warns.\par \par \uc2 "If you want to use the State to satisfy unlimited needs with scarce resources, you will end up with a completely ruined country", he explains.\par \par \uc2 The State has to support groups that were left out of progress, but not with the logic that every need is a right", he insists and concludes: "in Argentina, everything is a right and that is why the country is broken". "When taxes are high, productivity tends to fall", he analyzes. "Taxes have to be as low as possible and that gives a gigantic incentive for the creation of wealth", he stresses. "The classical liberal tradition allows us to argue in favor of a limited state that charges very low taxes," he points out. "It is very important that the state does not affect the right to property and protects it," he warns. "When there is no competition and there is private property, there is a fascistization of the system," he analyzes. "Mercosur should not matter and should be integrated, as Chile has done, to different economic blocs", he compares.\par \par \uc2 "Democracy is not an end in itself", he warns,\par \par \uc2 "Democracy can produce a frightening result", he warns. "Democracy has to be liberal to be worthwhile," he argues. "Compared to Bolsonaro, the progressive left put in religious criteria of this postmodern morality that is completely destructive and pathological," he posits. "If you have the right to life, you have the right to bear arms and defend your life," he asserts. "Boric's government wants to take all the weapons away from citizens while we have the highest homicide rate in the history of Chile", he specifies.\par \par \uc2 "The discourse against guns seems to me the most dangerous", he assures.\par \par \uc2 The respected Chilean intellectual Axel Kaiser, one of the most influential voices in the Latin American liberal and libertarian world, was in La Repregunta. Kaiser is president of the Foundation for Progress in Chile. He holds a PhD in Philosophy and Political Economy from the University of Heidelberg, Germany, and is a lawyer from the Universidad Diego Portales, Chile. He is the author of numerous books, the latest, El economista callejero. 15 lessons in economics to survive politicians and demagogues, presented by Javier Milei in Buenos Aires.\par \par \uc2 When libertarian ideas gain ground in this election year, questions arise: Does every libertarian want to dynamite the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Are we all libertarians, including Javier Milei? Why do libertarians like Trump? Kaiser made his analysis.\par \par \uc2 In the libertarian utopia, can there be a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -For a country to achieve libertarian ideas in the economic field, does it necessarily have to dynamite its \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -There is a great discussion among economists of libertarian inspiration also in this matter. But there is a consensus among libertarians that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be eliminated. The reason why it should be eliminated is that it has a task that is impossible to do, which is price calculation. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and let us not forget that this is one of the proposals in the Communist Manifesto among the 10 points of Marx and Engels, not only determines the quantity of money but also, at the same time, the rate of interest. There are conflicting ideas among economists on this matter but those who are closer to the libertarian or classical liberal school propose the abolition of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its replacement by a system of calibre banking, competition of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and, in countries like ours, dollarization which, ultimately depends, of course, on a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is in the United States. At least, it puts a brake on our central banks. The problem is that, in Latin America, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is used politically to finance public spending. Then you have gigantic inflations like those in Argentina or Venezuela. Throughout history, in the whole region, we have had periods of shortages and inflation. But there is a deeper point, in addition to the discussion about the independence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which also reaches the United States and Europe because they do not seem to be independent there either. The more fundamental problem is that the Central Banks cannot make the calculation they are supposed to make of how much money the economy needs at any given minute and what the level of interest rates should be. That is the reason why socialism fails: because they cannot calculate prices. The price system is an extremely complex system. That is why Central Banks do not know how to allocate resources from one side to the other, how much of each thing is missing... This is a process that is determined by the demand and supply of products among us, human beings who exchange every day. And with money it should be no different. That is a more technical argument. The political argument is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ends up being used by the mafia that governs our countries to finance public spending and ends up destroying the quality of life, especially for the poorest people\par \par \uc2 -In Argentina, we know this version of a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tied to political decisions and financing the fiscal deficit. But this is not the case in all the economies of the region. In Chile it has been independent. In Peru, despite the enormous political instability, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , headed by Julio Velarde, has been the stable economic authority that has been allowing a reasonable Peruvian economy in fiscal terms and with growth.\par \par \uc2 Italso shows positive results in Brazil, where it has managed to maintain a certain independence from political power by setting certain rules. Is the problem a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  co-opted by politics and not the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general? Could there be a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that functions with that independence that is required to fulfill its role in a liberal society or in a liberal or libertarian political organization?\par \par \uc2 There are \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  models that are certainly more responsible than others. And it has a lot to do with the idiosyncrasies of each country. In Chile, the history of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is disastrous. It is a history of chronic inflation until the late 80's and early 90's when a much more independent institution was set up, anchored, moreover, in the Chilean Constitution, which prohibits the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from buying public debt. In the new Constitution, they wanted to eliminate that restriction. A couple of exceptions were introduced, but it was one of the strictest constitutions in the world in terms of how our \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  operates. It is totally forbidden to finance the government. If a country has something like that and a more sensible and more responsible political class, capable of enforcing that independence, and a technocratic culture that goes after that goal, that country could have a better functioning \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Clearly, the case of Chile is an example. But even so, we still have double digit inflation. Chile is not in the best of all worlds in terms of monetary policy either.\par \par \uc2 better than a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be to have a system of free issuance, free banking, as we had in the past. It worked much better than the history of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Better than aCentral Bank would be to have a system of free issuance, free banking, as\par \par \uc2 we had in the past.It worked much better than the history of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the United States, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also finances the government deficit and the public debt. So does the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They are an embarrassment from the point of view of their independence. That is why they also have rampant inflation and high levels of debt. Now, Argentina cannot have an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it will never be allowed that independence. The only way out is dollarization, as Ecuador, Panama, El Salvador did. Because the political class, the Argentine elites, are predatory, they form interest groups that will always do everything possible to exploit the rest with the resources at their disposal and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very tempting for politicians. In Chile, we almost lost the independence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the new Constitution because our politicians wanted to get their hands on the banknote printer.\par \par \uc2 Are we all castes and so is Javier Milei?-In his vision of the Argentine political class as very focused on rapacity, the notion of caste that Javier Milei insists on is implicit. Now, there are problems with that notion of caste: who defines who is a caste and what is outside the caste?\par \par \uc2 -Yes, it is a political argument. Political arguments are difficult to qualify. When you are in a campaign in a country that is suffering economic ravages, as Argentina is, you cannot make the most sophisticated, academic analysis. I recently spent a couple of months living in Argentina. But what is clear is that Argentina is what in political economy is called a "limited access order", that is, it is a rent-seeking society, a parasitic society where all interest groups try to live at the expense of everyone else. A dynamic of extractivism is generated from the connections that certain groups have with the State. And I am not only referring to businessmen who expect all production to be national and free trade to be closed, or to politicians who live off the State and who employ their friends and who, in addition, do all kinds of corrupt things with public works. There are also sectors of society that get used to receiving transfers from the State and end up being a captive vote. So, which is the caste? Those who have decision-making power and who, instead of governing for the common good, govern for their own interest and the interest of those who support them. This is something very old in philosophy. Aristotle distinguished between different governments. When the monarchy ceases to be concerned with the common good and becomes an instrument at the service of those in power, it becomes a tyranny. The same happens with aristocracy: when it no longer cares about the common good, it is called oligarchy. And what you have today in Argentina is an awful oligarchy that has destroyed the country. This context means that if you do not play within those rules, even if you do not want to, you cannot survive. And that is the big problem Argentina has. And the only one who denounces it as it should be is Javier Milei\par \par \uc2 -I completely understand the point, but the problem of the definition and scope of the concept of caste still remains. One of the reproaches made to Milei, for example, is his relationship with businessman \uc2 Eduardo Eurnekian\uc2 , to whom he provided professional services for some years. In the composition of place that you raise, he could be considered as one of these businessmen who advocate for their interests in society unbalancing that more virtuous process of a libertarian or liberal society. So, again, is there anything left outside the caste? That question persists because not even a libertarian like Milei can show a trajectory free from the game of caste. That is, a caste society has, in a way, castes everywhere, as is clear from his explanation. It is a structural problem of\par \par \uc2 society-what defines caste is not that it seeks its interest. Everything was looking for our interest and that's fine. Everywhere in the world, even in the most economically free countries like New Zealand and Switzerland, businessmen, of course, look for their interest. We all do. We go to work because we want an income. That's the normal thing to do. The problem is when you use influence and the power of the State, from networks built in an institution that is supposed to serve the common good, to serve itself. As far as I know, Javier Milei has never done that\par \par \uc2 ,but if he works for a business that works according to those rules, isn't that a questionable point?\par \par \uc2 No, it depends on the company in question. I don't know it. I don't have the slightest idea of the details. But in the end one lives in an ecosystem. So, by that logic, we would all be entering a caste whether we want to or not. But\par \par \uc2 thatis exactly the point that my question questions: that when someone defines that there is a caste in society, that systemic structure of castes ends up placing us all within a caste, including the libertarian who fights against\par \par \uc2 caste-No, because you are not exercising influence and power so that this parasitic system deepens, remains and benefits you-If\par \par \uc2 someone advises a prominent member of this functioning, he is contributing to consolidate\par \par \uc2 it- We would have to look at the specific case. The\par \par \uc2 definition of caste has to do with the definition of oligarchy: it is a small group of people who have closed the system. They have closed the system to benefit themselves at the expense of others and the rest of us have no choice but to live in that ecosystem, or we have to leave the country.\par \par \uc2 -Let's leave the personal cases. Let's talk in general. Let us suppose that there is a business sector that functions as a caste, as you describe it, and there is a politician who claims to be anti-caste and who has worked advising this business sector so that its profitability grows. Is that politician not contributing to the consolidation of a caste system?\par \par \uc2 Not necessarily, because a person who is working for that businessman manufacturing something or cleaning the company's corridors is not contributing to a caste system. The definition of caste has to do with the definition of oligarchy: it is a small group of people who have closed the system. They have closed the system to benefit themselves at the expense of others and the rest of us have no choice but to live in that ecosystem, or we have to leave the country.\par \par \uc2 But one thing is an anonymous worker, who is very far from the apex of the pyramid of that caste, and another very different thing is an advisor on economic and business issues. In direct dialogue with the vertex of the pyramid of that caste, that advisor is consolidating the perpetuation and growth of that caste\par \par \uc2 . It depends on the advice you give. If you are lobbying for that company to benefit from the State, to be given subsidies, for example, or special privileges or to be protected from competition, you are clearly helping to consolidate the caste. But I have absolutely no doubt in my mind that Javier Milei has never done anything like that. If you are simply doing accounting or marketing analysis, you are not making any contribution to consolidate the caste. What you are doing is that a productive unit more or less sustains itself, especially with the problem of legislation and regulations that exist at the State level. In a corrupt system like that, after all, there are still jobs. Therefore, the way to solve the problem of that functioning is to go upwards and change the regulations. All this is produced only and exclusively by State regulations: only by that. If there were free trade in Argentina, 70% of the caste would disappear immediately. If there were transparent systems for awarding public works, another percentage would disappear. Chile is 29th in the ranking of economic freedom; we used to be much higher, but we have been breaking it. Even so, we have problems, but as it is a country so integrated to the world from the free trade point of view, we can handle it. Argentina is among the last 10 or 20 in the world in terms of economic freedom. When a country has a system of institutions that is completely corrupt, that has to be fixed at the level of the Constitution and at the level of legislation. And the only way to do that is to have a government willing to make those changes and a citizenry willing to face the cost. Because there is a cost to pay for a while, at least, so that the country can take off afterwards. That is what Chile did. In the early 1970s, Chile was one of the poorest countries in Latin America because we had a caste led by socialists and led by people from the right. That caste extracted the wealth of the country, did not allow free trade; there was a system of import substitution, all national production. Finally, in 1973, we ended up going bankrupt in an inflation and we know the rest of the story. We have to be careful with the analysis. If they do not make a deep, institutional transformation, Argentina is completely lost.Wherethere is a need, is there a right?-A key discussion in Argentina and in the world, and which particularly crosses libertarian ideas, has to do with the role of the State and the role of the market. In El economista callejero, you explain the issue of scarcity of resources versus the logic of the expansion of rights. Could you develop the arguments on that point?\par \par \uc2 -Yes, it is very important for the discussion in Argentina, a country I love and admire very much. There is no awareness among citizens in general, nor among the political elite and many people in the media that there is a basic principle: that there are scarce resources. Those scarce resources have to be produced by people who go to work, who invest, develop their talent and risk their capital. Therefore, if you want to temper that scarcity of resources, you have to provide all the incentives to make it easy for the people who produce them. If you tax them too much, put regulations that kill them, distort the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because politicians spend more than they have and finance them with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the result will be the depredation of resources. Therefore, the human capital and wealth of that country will flee. In Chile, an example that is not as dramatic as Argentina's, in the last few years, there have been 80 billion dollars of capital flight. Why? Because we have started to follow a path more similar to that of Argentina. In Argentina there is much more that could be inside and is not. Those dollars would generate employment, innovation, increase wages, we would all be better off. They would pay more taxes for production and economic activity than what they are generating. It is a fallacy to say, as Eva Per\u243\'00\'F3n said, that where there is a need, a right is born. Needs are not rights. And as long as Argentines do not get out of their heads that Peronist mental infection with the idea that "I have the right to get everything for free", they will not get ahead. That conception is a call to populisms of all colors to distribute and kill the goose that lays the golden eggs.\par \par \uc2 They will bemuch better off getting ahead by their own means in an environment of freedom\par \par \uc2 - I am conceptually interested: why is need not a right? I imagine a child born in poverty, whose parents do not have enough money to feed him/her because they were born in that poverty that reproduces itself. Is that need for food not a right of childhood?\par \par \uc2 Let's suppose you have a very well-to-do neighbor who lives in the same neighborhood and sends her children to the most expensive school in Argentina. That's her lifestyle. It always has been until one day she goes bankrupt and can no longer afford the school or the luxurious apartment, which for her and her family is a necessity.\par \par \uc2 Ithas been her way of life all her life. Does she have a right to take part of your income to finance her children's schooling?\par \par \uc2 No: one thing are unsatisfied basic needs and another thing are needs that are close to a luxury\par \par \uc2 - it is not a luxury to give your children a good education. It is an ideal that we all want to achieve. In other words, public schools are a disaster in Argentina. You can see it in the results of international tests. Now, the question is: can anything be done to help that family? Yes, first of all, economic growth: poverty can be overcome with economic growth, not with the distribution that has been going on for 80 years in Argentina and that leads to a poverty rate of 45 percent,\par \par \uc2 but the problem of economic growth cannot be reversed quickly\par \par \uc2 :economic growth is improved by changing three things that the oligarchies in Argentina do not want to change in order to achieve it. Argentina was the richest country in the world in terms of per capita income in 1896. There was no redistributing welfare state. And poverty was falling at a rate never seen before in the history of mankind until then. So don't tell me that Argentina needs the State to lift people out of poverty. The State is the cause of people being in poverty in Argentina. Going back to your point, of course there can be limited and focused aid policies. But we must not confuse targeted policies with the idea that every need is a right. You may have a need for an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  that costs 500 million dollars and you can only have it done at the \uc2 Mayo Clinic\uc2  in the U.S. Do you have the right to have the whole world finance 500 million dollars for you to have the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  there? If you continue with that logic, you end up with a country like Argentina, completely ruined\par \par \uc2 -then the problem is the expansion of that notion to other types of rights. One could establish basic rights such as food, the right to education, two rights, for example.\par \par \uc2 -No. There could be education systems financed by vouchers, with taxpayers' money. I resist a mentality: to think that you have a right because you need something. Because the notion of need is extremely broad and endless. One of the rules of economics is that resources are scarce while needs are unlimited. If you use the state to satisfy unlimited needs with scarce resources, you are going to end up in a completely ruined country.\par \par \uc2 If there were two basic needs, education and food for children and adolescents, would you agree that that is reasonable in a liberal society?\par \par \uc2 I agree that there are margins in which you can use taxpayers' money, because we pay for it with our taxes, to support groups that have been left out of a process of general progress when you have high economic growth and capital investment. In that case, yes, you could do that. But I don't agree with justifying it as a right because it becomes unstoppable: why education and food and not health or clothing\par \par \uc2 -those would also be rights-\par \par \uc2 and why not transportation, housing, vacations or retirement? They have all that in Argentina and that is why the country is broken, because everything is a right in Argentina. So, the mentality that it is a right is different from saying: here there is a problem, let's look for a specific public policy and help to solve that problem without using it, in addition, to win votes and generate welfare networks, one of the main problems you also have in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 : the destruction of labor morale with state \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionism\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And you can see this even in cases like Sweden. When the state starts giving things away, people lose the work ethic. Even if they have opportunities to work, many people, not all, but many people lose the work ethic.\par \par \uc2 High taxes, a perverse logic.-In The Street Economist, you make a very refined reading of the incentives to go out to work and make an effort and increase the wealth of a household.What are those incentives and how, according to your perspective, high taxes undermine that incentive.\par \par \uc2 -If we are taxed 100 percent on our income, we are going to stop going to work and we are not going to produce anything, zero. I'm taking the example to the extreme. In other words, there is a point at which, when taxes are levied, productivity tends to fall. From that point on, you cannot collect more taxes than you are collecting because productivity starts to fall, and therefore, the stock of wealth in society falls and, therefore, less employment starts to be generated, you have more unemployment, more poor people, and so on. And also, the State collects less because, as you have less economic activity, less taxes are paid, so it can also make less social policy, so you have to indebt the State. And when you can't get it into debt, you have to go out and use the banknote printer. And people face inflation of around 100 percent. Taxes have to be low, as low as possible. That is going to give a gigantic incentive for wealth creation, for people to come from all over the world to invest in your country, for people to get ahead, to hire, to have entrepreneurship. That is the formula for success. It is true that there are European countries with high taxes, but notice that corporate taxes in countries like Sweden, Denmark and others are lower than in most Latin American countries: they charge 21 percent, while in Chile we are almost 45 percent\par \par \uc2 .A liberal State could charge taxes if there was an appropriate measure that would not produce collateral effects such as the disincentives you point out?\par \par \uc2 The classical liberal tradition, indeed, allows arguing in favor of a very limited State that charges very low taxes, with very general and open regulations for entrepreneurship, investment and innovation and with a very limited redistribution. Now, the anarcho-capitalist vision, no: the anarcho-capitalist vision affirms that the State is the problem because it acts on the basis of aggression on third parties. And that is true and that is why we have to keep it limited. Because what is the essence of the State? It is the monopoly of physical violence, that is what defines the State from the sociological point of view. They don't ask you for taxes, please: you pay them or they put you in jail or confiscate you to pay fines. The state, which is the political group that controls the public apparatus, penalizes you. They have the power to extract from your pocket. In this respect, classical liberalism is a slightly more pragmatic position than anarcho-capitalism, which is absolutely principled.\par \par \uc2 From the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to privateproperty-is this very reduced State accompanied by a notion of markets which, by its virtuous logic, regulates itself autonomously?\par \par \uc2 No, there has to be a fundamental regulatory framework, for example, the right to property. The State establishes a property right that is safeguarded in the Constitution at a high level and by the legislations. That is already a regulatory framework that establishes that you have the right to private property; if someone takes it away from you by force, he commits a crime; or you have the right to exchange it. Having that regulation, you can add other very general ones. Ideally, people, who know that what they have is theirs, will exchange it with other people and will enter into what we call the market. As Adam Smith said in The Wealth of Nations, there is a natural tendency of man and that is to exchange. Besides, we want to survive and improve our quality of life and that of our family. That is why we undertake and get ahead. With this type of general regulations, we will do well. It is very important that the state does not affect the right to property and protects it\par \par \uc2 - I completely agree with the institution of private property and how important it is to protect it. To challenge the logic of the most extreme libertarian ideas, just as there is a vogue for overthrowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it does not fulfill its role and distorts the functioning of a society, why don't libertarians argue that private property should also be overthrown in cases of corrupt businessmen who collide all the time with the State in spurious alliances in order to maximize their profits?\par \par \uc2 Within a system that protects private property, rules that open competition are also needed. That is another important element: when there is no competition and there is private property, the system becomes fascistized. Fascism, a system very similar to what Argentina has today, is a corporatist system. This was very strongly introduced by Per\u243\'00\'F3n in Argentina. It implies that unions, businessmen and politicians agree to close the system to their benefit. So, there can be private property, and that is better than having a Soviet-style economy where everybody is starving like Cuba or North Korea, but it is a lousy system. Private property assumes, as a principle, that all people inside and outside a country can freely dispose of their property and exchange it. When limits are placed on international exchange with industrial protectionism for national production, the exercise of the right to private property of both Argentines, who cannot exchange what they produce for something that someone else freely produced abroad, and the right to private property of foreigners, who cannot send you their product so that you can exchange it\par \par \uc2 ,\par \par \uc2 is being restricted.\par \par \uc2 When the State does not function in a virtuous way, it is planned to overthrow it but there is no question of overthrowing the right to private property in cases where that private property is the result of complicity with a corrupt caste.\par \par \uc2 -What happens is that private property is a general principle and therefore it cannot be overthrown because there are people who take advantage of regulations created by the State which is the one that allows them to abuse private property. What can be done in case of collusion is, for example, to sanction the persons responsible. If we follow your approach, it is like saying that if there is a soccer player who dopes, the problem is soccer and soccer players. The doping player is sanctioned, but the rest of the game goes on with the rules, which are transparent and honest,\par \par \uc2 but the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not sanctioned, it is brought down, and the corrupt capitalist is not,\par \par \uc2 butthe soccer referee who is corrupted to favor a team is removed. And that is the example of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 : it is not a private entity, it is a state entity and it is not acting neutrally.\par \par \uc2 Itis playing the game in favor of the politicians who control it\par \par \uc2 : with that logic of the libertarians, the company is playing in favor of its own enrichment. Shouldn't private property be taken away, following that logic:\par \par \uc2 theproblem is systemic when you have a country in which everything is closed and you have no international competition. I insist, Argentina must open up to the world. Mercosur should not matter and it should integrate, as Chile has done, to different economic blocs. Chile is among the most integrated countries. There you would see how they are doing: much better. The problem is systemic. The problem is not the businessman who colludes. If the businessman is given the signal that the law obliges him to collude or that he earns more if he colludes, it is obvious that he is going to do it. We have seen it in Chile. Before, they were all colluding because the law ends up forcing you to take that path because there was no international competition.\par \par \uc2 Libertarians, are they in favor of democracy or not so much?-When one listens to libertarians in Argentina, it is difficult to be clear about their assessment of liberal republican democracy. When Milei was asked, for example, if he believes in democracy, he answered: "I believe that democracy has a lot of errors, for example, I ask you the question the other way around: do you know Arrow's impossibility theorem? What is Arrow's impossibility theorem and what does this theorem imply for liberal democracy\par \par \uc2 ?The problem derived from Arrow's impossibility theorem is that it is difficult for democracy to end up being a faithful reflection of the preferences and wills of the majorities that are supposed to be represented in the elections. It is impossible to really arrive at a democratic system in strict rigor, in which the wills of those who have voted are represented. But it seems to me that democracy, and this is a very old tradition in classical liberalism, is not an end in itself. If democracy is understood as the electoral system by which the majority decides, it can produce appalling results, a dictatorship of the majority over the minorities, for example, and it can produce concentration camps. To be worthwhile, democracy has to be liberal. It has to go with that liberal name. Liberalism has to be a substantial ingredient to democracy. Otherwise, democracy has no advantage over any dictatorship of the worst kind. If a democracy does not respect human rights, fundamental rights, it becomes an excuse not only to concentrate power but also to create a system of plundering and robbery as it is in Argentina. In such cases, people will ask themselves at some point why they have to trust in such a system, which only exploits them. I am a firm believer in liberal democracy. The Constitution guarantees fundamental rights and restrains the power of the State and also that of the majority or organized minorities to prevent them from taking away fundamental rights such as property or the right to life and liberty. If this does not happen, without this liberal surname, democracy has no advantage over tyrannical systems. There must also be a system of freedoms that allow progress because otherwise it frustrates the great majorities. Democracy is destroyed by annihilating economic freedom: people do not want to live under a system that condemns them to misery, poverty and prevents them from getting ahead. That's also part of liberal democracy.\par \par \uc2 From Bolsonaro to Trump-Whydo libertarians feel more comfortable with illiberal right-wing populism like that of \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 -Idon't know if all libertarians feel more comfortable. But also, more comfortable compared to what? If you compare \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with Bernie Sanders, clearly you are going to be more comfortable with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because Sanders is a socialist who admires Cuba, beyond all the criticisms he makes of Trump. And that's not just true for libertarians; it's also true for the majority of Americans who voted for him. If you prefer someone, you prefer him to someone else, that is, Bolsonaro or Lula, Bolsonaro or Cristina\par \par \uc2 -I put it to you in these terms: Bolsonaro or Fernando Henrique Cardoso?\par \par \uc2 -It depends on public policies. There has been a lot of hysteria about Bolsonaro. What happened to many of his supporters when they entered government palaces was disastrous. There are also very brainless people behind these movements. But it all depends on what he stands for. Trump was going to be the great demon who was going to create World War III, he was going to put everybody in concentration camps and he didn't do any of that. He lowered taxes, he lowered regulations. And he's probably going to be the next president of the United States. I've never been a fan of Trump but if you give me a choice between a Democratic administration that has brought us almost to the brink of theThird World War with Russia, because it has been handled very badly according to several experts; it also has the worst migratory crisis in decades, which Trump had solved; it also has a debt issue, which Trump also left badly, obviously I do not vote for the person but I vote for the ideas and practices that he will carry out and each thing in its own context. America has strong institutions. It is completely absurd to think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to become a dictator in the US. There is nothing more ridiculous than to hear people on the left say that. The U.S. has strong institutions that would make such a thing impossible. And that was demonstrated in all the somewhat ridiculous claims that Trump made that they had the election stolen from them: he had to leave and now he's trying to come back. And if he comes back, it will be just another government and he will not be able to concentrate power in his hands. In the case of Bolsonaro, everybody said he was going to be a kind of Mussolini. What did Bolsonaro do so seriously? Did he persecute homosexuals, as it was said that he was going to put them in concentration camps? No, he did not do that.\par \par \uc2 -WithBolsonaro, a discourse came to Brazil that introduced religious criteria in personal decisions of the family and individuals. The\par \par \uc2 progressive left did much worse because it introduced religious criteria of this postmodern morality that is completely destructive and pathological and turned it into legislation everywhere. In part, Brazilians rebelled against that too when they elected Bolsaron. Let's not forget that he didn't lose by much the last lesson. And Lula is a criminal, an organized crime guy. On the issue of gun carrying, I am all for it. Decent and honest people have the right to defend themselves. If you have the right to life, you have the right to defend your life. The other thing is to let criminals enter your house, rape your children, massacre you and you cannot defend yourself. In Chile, the Boric government and the Communist Party, which to this day openly defends Cuba and Venezuela, want to take away all weapons from citizens. Meanwhile, we have the biggest security crisis in history and the highest homicide rate in Chilean history. And Boric himself has to surround himself with Carabineros because the popularity of the Armed Forces and Carabinero is soaring in the polls and Boric's is plummeting. In this context, they want to leave us at the mercy of the criminals. What good is it that the Constitution guarantees the right to life when your police do not work and, on top of that, the same State that does not protect you forbids you to defend yourself? The discourse against guns seems to me to be most dangerous, especially in countries like ours. Obviously, it has to be regulated and limits have to be set. Not just anyone should be able to have weapons, not just any caliber\par \par \uc2 ofweapon\par \par \uc2 . Education. PhD in Philosophy and Political Economy from the University of Heidelberg in Germany\par \par \uc2 ? Professional development. He is president of the Foundation for Progress in Chile\par \par \uc2 ? Books. His new book is El economista callejero. 15 lessons of economics to survive politicians and demagogues; he also wrote: The Pope and Capitalism and The Neoinquisition, among others.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230417ej4g001hn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
