\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The Fed's lock-up generates doubts in all markets\b0\par\par\uc2 1,126 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Luis Varela\par \par \uc2 eleconomista.com.ar\par \par \uc2 The reopening of the markets came with two major news items: The US has just announced that it created fewer jobs in March than in February (236,000 jobs against 311,000 the previous month). And the $300 agricultural dollar started (it will continue until May 31 for soybeans and until August 30 for other regional economy products).\par \par \uc2 This comes after the banking collapse in the US and Europe, with a massive distribution of dollars by the Fed to stop a domino fall of entities. And Massa's new agricultural dollar expects foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidation for US$ 9 billion to add reserves, but exports will take away product supply in the domestic market, at a time when the drought left a pronounced shortage of supply.\par \par \uc2 More inflationary pressures\par \par \uc2 What is the combination of helicopter raining dollars in the US and product supply shortages in Argentina? Simple: both decisions, here and in the north, will generate another inflationary pressure. And this scourge that is flooding the world with poor people and global tension was encouraged last week by another chess move in the world war in which the planet is submerging: the oil producing countries added more fire to the fuse.\par \par \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , which controls 40 percent of the world's oil supply, was on the way to cut supply by two million barrels per day as of May. However, several of its members announced additional "voluntary" reductions, i.e. without a binding agreement, which will run until the end of 2023. In total, over and above what they were already going to cut, Saudi Arabia, Iraq, UAE, Kuwait, Kazakhstan, Algeria and Oman will withdraw no less than 1.15 million barrels per day from supply.\par \par \uc2 What is the impact of this shutdown? Last week the WTI variant finished at US$ 80.47 (up from US$ 65.60 in mid-March) and the Brent variant finished at US$ 84.94 (up from US$ 71.40). Since mid-March, crude oil has risen by an average of 20% worldwide, and so far in April it has risen by 6.3%.\par \par \uc2 In other words, we find ourselves with more dollars in the US to save the banks, less supply of products in Argentina and global oil prices, which, according to experts, will mean upward inflationary pressure everywhere. Within the US, the CPI had peaked at 9% per year and due to the Fed's rate hike it flattened to 6% per year in the last data of February, but this week it will be known how much prices rose in March.\par \par \uc2 The same thing will happen in Argentina: next Friday, at 4:00 p.m., after the market close, the Indec will release the national CPI. The consulting firms that have already made their calculations have been quite heated, with an average estimate of 7.5%, the Creebba of Bah\u237\'00\'EDa Blanca calculated no less than 8.1%, the index of the City of Buenos Aires measured 7.1%. And the REM (Relevamiento de Expectativas de Mercado - Market Expectations Survey) prepared by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave 7% for March (and 110% annual rate).\par \par \uc2 The direction of rates\par \par \uc2 All these data, which seem to be separate indicators, have just turned a key global thermometer. Before the banking collapse and the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  adjustment, the US long rate had been falling, but last week's short rate has just turned around. In October 2022 the US 10-year rate reached 4.3% p.a. (a 12-year high), from there it dropped to 3.25% and now it has just bounced back to 3.4%.\par \par \uc2 With a latest inflation figure of 6% p.a. and with the recent massive distribution of dollars for the equivalent of 1.5 of Argentina's GDP, investors believe that US inflation will be a tough nut to crack. A 3.4% rate against 6% inflation is a negative investment, hence last week's growing abandonment of the global dollar, at a time when China is also seeking to de-dollarize the world by handing out swaps left and right.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the short week prior to Easter showed a general weakening of the greenback: down 1.1% against the Swiss franc, 0.7% against the pound, 0.5% against the yen and the euro and 0.1% in Brazil, all down. The dollar only remained unchanged against the yuan and rose 2.7% in Chile, with a flood of Chileans traveling to Mendoza for the long holiday, but not only for tourism: they took the trunks of their cars full of things they bought in supermarkets 40% cheaper on this side of the Andes.\par \par \uc2 The challenge of reservations\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meanwhile, a completely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  loses an average of US$100 million per day with its reserves runaway.\par \par \uc2 Before knowing the fine print of the small print of the agro dollar, analysts began to put numbers to the scope that this new patch of Economy may have. And they calculated that in the first 45 days of the agricultural dollar (between the dollars coming in from exports with a better exchange rate and imports that continue at full steam due to an official dollar at least 35 points behind) there will be a net increase of US$ 3 billion in reserves and a net issuance of $1 billion.\par \par \uc2 But the issue is inflation. Dairy farmers warned that the agro dollar will make domestic inputs more expensive, which will cause an increase in the costs to feed the cows, so the opening of April came with two surprises: strong rises in the price of milk, increases in cases of complaints about the arrival of watered milk and food companies that withdraw the supply of milk at Fair Prices, deciding to use it to produce hard cheeses, with which they get more profitability.\par \par \uc2 More weights in the market\par \par \uc2 As a result, a number of voices from the countryside (from organizations and producers) came out to say that the agricultural dollar that is now starting as the third version of the soybean dollar is "bread for today and hunger for tomorrow", since this preferential exchange rate generates more inflation and does not solve the underlying problems. Moreover, it will flood the domestic market with newly issued pesos.\par \par \uc2 And here are the key questions of the moment: where will the new pesos that will enter the market go? Will they be placed in fixed term deposits with negative rates? And analysts affirm that they will go to more consumption (more inflation) and at the end of the goods exchange channel they will end up becoming more demand for dollars.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230411ej4b004w8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
