\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Rtgs: Argentine Banks\u8217\'20\'19 Capitalization Mitigates Central Government Exposure\b0\par\par\uc2 2,051 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2023\par \uc2 Fitch Rating / Non Rating Action Commentary\par \uc2 FITRA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 by Fitch Ratings Inc., Fitch Ratings Ltd. and its subsidiaries. \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -Santiago/New York-28 February 2023: The increased risk posed to Argentina\u8217\'20\'19s banking system from public sector exposure is viewed negatively from a credit perspective and could become a ratings constraint over the medium term, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  says. However, \uc2 Fitch\uc2 -rated banks have ample capital cushions that mitigate the risk of growing central government exposure.\par \par \uc2 We expect financial and credit pressures to intensify for Argentina\u8217\'20\'19s banking system, as\par \par \uc2 mounting vulnerabilities facing the sovereign : {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10223931"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10223931}}}\par \par \uc2 and the impending election cycle could hinder government domestic debt placements in 2023. The government has faced difficulties in extending peso debt maturities past the current election cycle, due to policy uncertainties and fears of another debt reprofiling (as occurred in 2019). It has had to rely increasingly on financial support from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other public entities.\par \par \uc2 However, the impact on rated banks\u8217\'20\'19 financials should not be material. While not in our sovereign base case, any reprofiling of domestic debt by the government via maturity extensions would not be overly detrimental for the banks given their meaningful levels of capitalization and low exposures to the federal government. The risk of any larger impact currently is reflected in the ratings for Argentine banks.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 -rated banks in Argentina have limited exposure to the USD118 billion of central government debt maturing in 2023, which is largely held by the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , state-owned banks, and ANSES, Argentina\u8217\'20\'19s national social security administration, as short-term liquidity is mostly allocated to BCRA instruments\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://e.infogram.com/b54d0970-8b6c-4da5-9fb6-2cc1b40c969b?src=embed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Argentina Debt Maturities}}}\par \par \uc2 Among \uc2 Fitch\uc2 -rated banks, \uc2 Banco Santander Rio\uc2  has the lowest exposure to the public sector as a percentage of assets, with \uc2 Banco Macro\uc2  at the lowest as a percentage of equity (but highest percentage of assets). These assets consist of mostly \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  securities, though the proportion of national government securities has grown significantly since 2020.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://e.infogram.com/cbac012c-406d-4ef4-bb4e-54e38c8f3624?src=embed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Argentina\b0 \highlight22  Rated Bank Public Exposure}}}\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations, very low credit growth and a lack of profitable alternatives have led banks to invest excess liquidity in public sector assets, with this exposure reaching 44% of the system\u8217\'20\'19s assets at YE2022 versus 35% in the year-ago period. We do not expect banks to materially reduce their sovereign exposure in the short term, given the attractive risk-adjusted returns and the backdrop of anemic loan growth.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://e.infogram.com/20b10769-6a87-4fc3-a1da-395da092d8f8?src=embed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Argentina Financial System Public Exposure}}}\par \par \uc2 Capital and liquidity levels are strong due to the reduction of loans to less than 30% of banks\u8217\'20\'19 balance sheets. Banks have heavily focused on collections and management of deposits and liquidity. Excess liquidity has mostly been placed in public sector securities, which are generally viewed as low risk investments relative to other domestic issuers.\par \par \uc2 The sector outlook for Argentine banks is deteriorating as the operating environment (OE) remains highly challenging. Total credit extended by the financial system has contracted meaningfully (in real terms) in recent years, as the long recession and adverse OE have weakened demand for credit and banks\u8217\'20\'19 appetite to lend, most of all in commercial and retail loans.\par \par \uc2 Bank profitability will remain under pressure due to slow loan growth, higher credit costs and additional margin pressure. Key factors pressuring bank \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  include regulatory \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as interest rate caps and floors, and operating cost increases due to sustained, high inflation and Argentine peso depreciation. Additional regulatory \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (e.g. directed lending) could also exacerbate pressure on banks\u8217\'20\'19 profitability.\par \par \uc2 The ratings of the four Fitch-rated banks in Argentina are currently constrained by the sovereign rating. Argentine bank ratings have been driven by the adverse OE and low sovereign rating, leaving little room for differentiation in ratings. Key downside sensitivities to the sovereign include policy and political developments or economic weakness that would put debt sustainability or \uc2 IMF\uc2  funding in question, further macro instability, erosion of international reserves, or failure to recover access to external market funding.\par \par \uc2 Related Content:\par \par \uc2 Feb. 1, 2023:\par \par \uc2 Argentina Avoids Default Event, but Faces Mounting Vulnerability : {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10223931"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10223931}}}\par \par \uc2 Nov. 20, 2020:\par \par \uc2 Increasing Public Sector Exposure a Credit Negative for LatAm Banks : {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10143945"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10143945}}}\par \par \uc2 May 30, 2019:\par \par \uc2 Mounting Political, Economic Risks Pressure Argentina Banks : {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10077206"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10077206}}}\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Theresa Paiz Fredel\par \par \uc2 Senior Director, Latin American Financial Institutions\par \par \uc2 Financial Institutions\par \par \uc2 +1 212 908-0534\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings, Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 300 West 57th Street\par \par \uc2 New York, NY 10019\par \par \uc2 Santiago Gallo\par \par \uc2 Senior Director, Latin American Non-Bank Financial Institutions\par \par \uc2 +(562) 2499-3320\par \par \uc2 Todd Martinez\par \par \uc2 Senior Director, Sovereign Ratings\par \par \uc2 +1 212 908 0897\par \par \uc2 Laura Kaster, CFA\par \par \uc2 Senior Director, Fitch Wire\par \par \uc2 North and South American Financial Institutions\par \par \uc2 +1 646 582-4497\par \par \uc2 Media Relations: Elizabeth Fogerty, New York, Tel: +1 212 908 0526, Email: elizabeth.fogerty@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 The above article originally appeared as a post on the Fitch Wire credit market commentary page. The original article can be accessed at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\uc2 . All opinions expressed are those of \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  (\uc2 Fitch\uc2 ) credit ratings are subject to certain limitations and disclaimers. Please read these limitations and disclaimers by following this link: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/underst