\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms \uc2 YPF\uc2 's Ratings at '\uc2 CCC\uc2 -'\b0\par\par\uc2 2,244 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2023\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Release date - 13022023\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed the Long-Term Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Ratings (IDRs) of \uc2 YPF S.A\uc2 . at '\uc2 CCC\uc2 -'.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has also affirmed \uc2 YPF\uc2 's outstanding senior unsecured notes at 'CCC-'/'RR4'. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  maintains the standalone credit profile (SCP) at 'b'.\par \par \uc2 YPF\uc2 's ratings are in line with \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's 'Government Related Entities Criteria.' \uc2 YPF\uc2  is majority owned by the government and strategically important to the country. \uc2 YPF\uc2 's dominant market share in the supply of liquid fuels in Argentina, coupled with its large hydrocarbon production footprint in the country, could expose the company to government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through pricing policies or investment strategies.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 Links to Sovereign: \uc2 YPF\uc2  is closely linked to the Republic of Argentina due to its ownership structure, as well as recent government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls the company through its 51% stake, and provincial government officials serve on the company's board of directors. In addition, the republic sometimes governs the company's strategy and business decisions.\par \par \uc2 The Argentine government has a history of significant interference in the oil and gas sector, particularly in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the company's ability to implement its pricing policy for refined products. SCP of \uc2 YPF\uc2  is in the 'b' category, as is reflective of the issuer's weak coverage ratios and a production cost profile that is above average compared to peers in the region.\par \par \uc2 Moderate Leverage; Tighter Coverage Ratios: \uc2 YPF\uc2  has maintained a moderate leverage profile. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  estimates \uc2 YPF\uc2 's total debt/EBITDA will be 1.2x in 2022 compared 1.9x in 2021 and increased to an average of 2.1x over the rated horizon. \uc2 YPF\uc2 's total debt/1P has materially improved. The company reported 1,143 mmboe in 2021, translating into a total debt to 1P of reserves of USD6.45boed in 2021 and an estimated USD5.86boe for 2022 for 1P reserves as reported in 2021.\par \par \uc2 The company's leverage profile has been stable, but the company is challenged by limited pool and high cost of capital given the macroeconomic environment in Argentina and its intrusive capital controls. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  estimates \uc2 YPF\uc2 's EBITDA to interest expense will be 7.3x in 2022 and fall to an average 4.4x thereafter, which is consistent with the b rating category.\par \par \uc2 Stable Production and High Cost Profile: \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's rating case assumes production will remain flat averaging 529,000boed over the rating horizon. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  estimates \uc2 YPF\uc2 's 2021 half-cycle costs of USD28.5boe and full-cycle cost of USD42.3boe, which are both high and above average for players in the region.\par \par \uc2 The company's high cost is mostly attributed to higher than average lifting cost of USD11.1boe and high interest cost per barrel of USD8.0boe in 2021, which has improved from USD12.0boe in 2019. The company's full-cycle break-even implied prices were above weighted average realization prices for oil and gas, primarily as a result of decreasing domestic gas prices and high level of gas production, which accounted for approximately 55% of total production together with natural gas liquids during 2021. Crude oil production comprised 45%.\par \par \uc2 Volatile Operating Environment: While the volatile economic environment in Argentina has made unconventional development in Vaca Muerta difficult, Argentina's limited USD reserves has incentivized the government to implement numerous policies to drive further investment in Vaca Muerta, most notably Plan Gas 4, which is designed to assure Argentina has adequate gas that is sourced domestically to avoid importing expensive liquid natural gas (LNG).\par \par \uc2 YPF\uc2  is well positioned to benefit for these policies that aim to shift Argentina from a net importer to a net exporter of crude and eventually of LNG. Nonetheless, the domestic economy is plagued high government debt to GDP ratio of 81% in 2022 and 2023, inflation that is estimated to reach nearly 100% in 2023, and low Real GDP growth of less than 0.5% for both 2023 and 2024, per \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's estimates. These factors make \uc2 YPF\uc2  and Argentine corporates highly exposed to economic disruption.\par \par \uc2 Impact of Capital Controls: Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  capital controls (A-7490) have been extended through 2023, with the possibility of yet another extension when the current one expires. The rule requires that all hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt maturities will need to refinance at least 60% of the outstanding debt in order to access the official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for up to 40% of the maturity, extending the maturities by at least two years.\par \par \uc2 This ruling has negatively impacted multiple issuers, including \uc2 YPF\uc2 , leading to numerous \uc2 Fitch\uc2  defined Distressed Debt Exchanges. Argentine corporates with USD debt maturities remain highly exposed to refinancing risks, which are heightened by the capital controls. Approximately 92% of \uc2 YPF\uc2 's debt is international and USD3.3 billion maturing between 2023-2025. The company reported a healthy cash balance of USD1,108 billion that is trapped in Argentina, and \uc2 YPF\uc2 's ability to service its maturities is limited.\par \par \uc2 Derivation Summary\par \par \uc2 YPF\uc2 's linkage to the sovereign is similar in nature to its Latin American national oil companies peers, namely \uc2 PEMEX\uc2  (BB-/Stable), \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  (BB-/Stable) and \uc2 Ecopetrol\uc2  (BB+/Stable), and government-owned entities ENAP (A-/Stable) and \uc2 Petroperu\uc2  (BB+/RWN). These companies all have strong linkage to their respective sovereigns given their strategic importance to each country and the potentially significant negative social and financial implication a default could have at a national level.\par \par \uc2 YPF\uc2 's upstream business closest peers are \uc2 Pemex\uc2 , \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  and \uc2 Ecopetrol\uc2 . \uc2 YPF\uc2 's total 2021 production averaged 467,000boed, and the reserve life was 6.7 years, most comparable with \uc2 Ecopetrol\uc2  with a production of 679,000boed and a reserve life of 8.1 years in 2021, but less than \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 ' production of 2.8 million boed and a reserve life of 9.8 years and \uc2 Pemex\uc2 's production at 2.5 million boed and a reserve life of 8.3 years.\par \par \uc2 YPF\uc2  has an adequate capital structure with gross leverage ratio defined as total debt/EBITDA of 1.9x in 2021 and total debt/1P of USD6.5 per boe compared with \uc2 Ecopetrol\uc2  at 1.4x and USD8.1 total debt/1P, \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  at 0.8x and USD3.6 per boe and \uc2 Pemex\uc2  at 14.6x and USD15.3 per boe.\par \par \uc2 Unlike its peers ENAP, \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 , \uc2 Pemex\uc2  and \uc2 Petroperu\uc2 , \uc2 YPF\uc2  is not the sole provider of refined fuels in Argentina. In 2021, the company had a 54% market share. \uc2 YPF\uc2  is an integrated energy company, similar to \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  and \uc2 Pemex\uc2 , offering the company more financial flexibility, while ENAP is predominately a refining company that sells to marketers. Historically, \uc2 YPF\uc2  has operated autonomously with periodic controls of fuel prices and crude. Similar to \uc2 Pemex\uc2  and \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 , \uc2 YPF\uc2  has administered an import-parity pricing policy and other price controls to help tame inflation. \uc2 YPF\uc2  has successfully been able to tighten spread and recuperate losses realized during period of pricing freezes.\par \par \uc2 When compared with downstream-focused entities ENAP and \uc2 Petroperu\uc2 , \uc2 YPF\uc2  leverage in 2021 level of 1.9x and 1.2x in 2022 is compares with ENAP at 5.5x and \uc2 Petroperu\uc2  at 26.9x. \uc2 Petroperu\uc2 's elevated leverage is explained by its investment plan to increase capacity by 2021, while ENAP has maintained a higher leverage profile for an extended period of time, but the company is highly strategic for the Chilean government, and thus its rating is aligned as a result.\par \par \uc2 Key Assumptions\par \par \uc2 Average gross production of 529,000boe from 2021-2024;\par \par \uc2 Realized oil price of USD63.7/bbl in 2022 and an average of USD50/bbl thereafter;\par \par \uc2 Natural gas prices rise to USD3.51/MMBTU in 2022 and settle at USD3.50/MMBTU thereafter;\par \par \uc2 Average annual capex of roughly USD2.9 billion per year from 2022-2026;\par \par \uc2 Downstream sales volume follows Real GDP forecasts and \uc2 YPF\uc2  is a net purchaser of crude;\par \par \uc2 Effective tax rate of 25%;\par \par \uc2 Fitch ARS/USD forecasts for year average and end of period during 2021-2022;\par \par \uc2 No dividend payments over the rate horizon.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 The Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR is linked to Argentina's sovereign rating, and an upgrade can only occur with an upgrade of Argentina's sovereign rating.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 Argentina's sovereign rating is '\uc2 CCC\uc2 -', the lowest level allowed under \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's sovereign criteria; therefore, a downgrade of Foreign and Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDRs would reflect \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's belief that a default of some kind appears probable, or a default or default-like process has begun for the company, represented by a 'CC' or 'C' rating.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Non-Financial Corporate issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Liquidity and Debt Structure\par \par \uc2 Liquidity Vulnerable to Capital Controls: \uc2 YPF\uc2  reported USD1.4 billion in cash and cash equivalents in 3Q22. Per \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's estimates, this covers roughly 1.7x the company's interest expense. The company faces significant refinancing risk, heighten by the capital controls, with USD941 million in debt maturing in 2023 followed by USD967 million in 2024 and USD1,421 million in 2025. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  expects \uc2 YPF\uc2  will continue to roll over short term bank and trade financing debt over the rated horizon.\par \par \uc2 Issuer Profile\par \par \uc2 YPF, S.A\uc2  is the largest fully integrated energy company in Argentina. \uc2 YPF\uc2  participates in three segments: Upstream, Downstream and Gas and Power.\par \par \uc2 The Upstream segment carries out all activities relating to the exploration, development and production of oil and natural gas. Upstream revenues are principally generated from the sale of oil to the Downstream segment, as well as the sale of produced natural gas to the Gas and Power segment. \uc2 YPF\uc2 's oil and gas production averaged approximately 504 kboe/d during 3Q2022, composed of 225 kbbl/d of oil (including condensates), 38kbbl/d of NGL and 38MM M3/d of natural gas. For YE 2021, the company reported proved reserves of 707 mm bbl of liquids and 463 mm boe of gas, which translates to 6.2 years of reserve life assuming 100% replacement rate in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The Downstream \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  include refining, marketing, transportation and distribution of oil and petroleum products, petroleum derivatives, petrochemicals, liquefied petroleum gas and bio-fuels. \uc2 YPF\uc2  sells 86% of its output domestically, while it exports the remaining 14%. YTD 9/22, the company refined 283 kbbl/d of oil, accounting for over 50% of Argentina's total refining capacity. The difference between oil production and oil refining is covered through domestic market purchases. \uc2 YPF\uc2  has the most gas stations in the country, and it has a 59.2% and 56.0% market share for diesel and gasoline sales, respectively.\par \par \uc2 The Gas and Power business unit was created in March 2016 and is responsible for the marketing and distribution of natural gas to third parties and the company's downstream segment, as well as the generation of conventional and renewable energy. Residential users consume 40% of the natural gas output; the balance is split between industrial users (31%) and power plants (29%).\par \par \uc2 YPF\uc2  is controlled by the Argentine government through its majority stake of 51% since 2012. Until 1993, most of the company's predecessors were state-owned with \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  dating back to the 1920's. Following privatization in that year, the Argentine government's equity stake was reduced from 100% to 20%.\par \par \uc2 From 1999 until 2012, the company was controlled by \uc2 Repsol\uc2  until the May 2012 Expropriation Law was passed by the Argentine Congress. Following the government's expropriation of \uc2 YPF\uc2 's controlling stake previously held by \uc2 Repsol\uc2 , 51% of the company's share capital is held by the Argentine government.\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\par \par \uc2 The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 YPF S.A\uc2 . has an ESG Relevance Score of '4' for Governance Structure due to \{DESCRIPTION OF ISSUE/RATIONALE\}, which has a negative impact on the credit profile, and is relevant to the rating[s] in conjunction with other factors.\par \par \uc2 YPF S.A\uc2 . has an ESG Relevance Score of '4' for GHG Emissions & Air Quality due to \{DESCRIPTION OF ISSUE/RATIONALE\}, which has a negative impact on the credit profile, and is relevant to the rating[s] in conjunction with other factors.\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\