\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Downgrades Numerous Corporate Issuers IDRs\b0\par\par\uc2 1,336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2022\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Release date - 03112022\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has downgraded the Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) and Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC) Issuer Default Ratings (IDR) of numerous corporate issuers as a result of \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's recent downgrade of Argentina's sovereign FC and LC IDR rating to '\uc2 CCC\uc2 -' from '\uc2 CCC\uc2 .'\par \par \uc2 Further, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has taken numerous rating actions on the rated securities of select issuers, assigning a higher recovery rating of 'RR3' from 'RR4', which resulted in either an upgrade or an affirmation of the rated issuance. The revised recovering ratings reflects historical precedents of numerous distressed debt exchanges done by Argentine corporates, that did not result in reduction in principal, and the recoveries were above the implied recovery of an 'RR3' (51%-70%), and the previous recovery rating of 'RR4' (31%-50%), even when considering the intrusive capital controls laws. As Per \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's Country-Specific Treatment of Recovery Criteria, when an issuer actually enters a distressed or defaulted state, such as Argentina (\uc2 CCC\uc2 -), \uc2 Fitch\uc2  can assign a higher recovery rating for an issuer instrument if it believes that recoveries in the individual case will be consistent with a higher recovery rating.\par \par \uc2 Please refer to the bottom of this press release for a complete list of all rating actions.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 Downgrade of Issuer Default Ratings: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has downgraded numerous Argentine corporates in line with the one notch downgrade of the FC and LC IDR of Argentina to '\uc2 CCC\uc2 -' from '\uc2 CCC\uc2 '.\par \par \uc2 The ratings of Argentine Utilities: AES Argentina Generacion S.A.,Generacion Mediterranea S.A., \uc2 Genneia S.A\uc2 ., and MSU Energy S.A., have been downgraded to '\uc2 CCC\uc2 -' from '\uc2 CCC\uc2 ' in line with the sovereign, as each issuer's revenues are highly exposed to the government. Argentine electricity utilities are exposed to CAMMESA, who depends on financial support by the Argentine government in the form of subsidies, representing nearly 80% of the total cost of the system.\par \par \uc2 YPF SA\uc2  ratings have been downgraded to '\uc2 CCC\uc2 -' form '\uc2 CCC\uc2 ' in line with the sovereign, as its ratings are linked to the Republic of Argentina due to its ownership structure, as well as historical precedents of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls the company through its 51% stake, and provincial government officials serve on the company's board of director. Per \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's assessment of the Government Related Entities criteria, the strategic incentive to support and impact of a default are high and the ratings are equalized, even though \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's considers the standalone credit profile of the company to be above that of the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 IRSA Inversiones y Representaciones S.A\uc2 . FC and LC IDR ratings were downgraded to '\uc2 CCC\uc2 -' from '\uc2 CCC\uc2 ' to reflect the company's exposure to Argentina. The company's real estate assets are all in Argentina and are subjected to the deteriorating macroeconomic environment of the sovereign. IRSAs is exposed to the devaluation of the Argentine peso compared to its U.S. dollar debt, and its challenges to maintaining its rental income in real terms given the macroeconomic environment.\par \par \uc2 CLISA-Compania Latinoamericana de Infraestructura y Servicios FC and LC IDR ratings were downgraded to '\uc2 CCC\uc2 -' from '\uc2 CCC\uc2 ' reflecting its tight liquidity profile, in great part due to its significant working capital needs from the prolonged period of repayments for its receivables in relation to its payables. The company usually manages this with a combination of short and long-term financing.\par \par \uc2 Arcor S.A.I.C\uc2 . and \uc2 Mastellone Hermanos Sociedad Anonima\uc2  LC IDR were downgraded one notch to 'B' from 'B+' and 'B' from 'B' following the LC downgrade of the sovereign. Their FC IDR's were affirmed at 'B' and 'B-' with a Stable Outlook.\par \par \uc2 Affirmed Ratings: The FC and LC ratings of Compania General de Combustibles S.A. (CGC) , \uc2 Pampa Energia S.A\uc2 ., \uc2 Capex S.A\uc2 ., and \uc2 Telecom Argentina S.A\uc2 . were affirmed, as were the FC IDRs of Arcor and Mastellone. The affirmations reflect their idiosyncratic businesses profiles that have proved to be resilient, over the years, to a deteriorating macroeconomic environment plagued with inflation and devaluation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The companies have maintained their strong leverage profile, liquidity and diversified cash flow, some of which are supported through business outside of Argentina and/or exports that have bode well for the credits.\par \par \uc2 In the case of \uc2 Pampa Energia\uc2 , CGC, \uc2 Telecom Argentina\uc2 , and Arcor their ratings are capped by the country ceiling of Argentina, which was affirmed at 'B-', but their credit profiles are consistent with a higher rating category.\par \par \uc2 Agua y Saneamientos Argentinos S.A\uc2 .'s (\uc2 AySA\uc2 ) ratings were affirmed at 'CC' to reflect \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's expectation that the company will announce some type of liability management exercise to remain in compliance with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Argentine Republic\uc2 's restrictions on a hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt refinancing that will likely be deemed a Distressed Debt Exchange (DDE).\par \par \uc2 Revised Recovery Ratings: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has revised the recovery ratings of numerous bonds to RR3 from RR4, given recent precedents in Argentina, where issuers launched DDEs in response to the intrusive capital control laws, which requires issuers refinance a minimum of 60% of their hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  principal in order to access the official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market controlled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Argentina.\par \par \uc2 None of the respective exchanges resulted in a reduction in principal, and the recoveries were above the implied recovery of an 'RR3' (51%-70%), and the previous recovery rating of 'RR4' (31%-50%). Per \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's Country-Specific Treatment of Recovery Criteria, when an issuer actually enters a distressed or defaulted state, such as Argentina (\uc2 CCC\uc2 -), \uc2 Fitch\uc2  can assign a higher recovery rating for an issuer instrument if it believes that recoveries in the individual case will be consistent with a higher RR, as is in this case.\par \par \uc2 The bond ratings of Arcor, Capex, \uc2 Pampa Energia\uc2  and \uc2 Telecom Argentina\uc2  were upgraded one notch to reflect the higher recovery rating of 'RR3', which allows the rating of the security to be one notch above the FC IDR.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, the bond rating of AES Argentina, \uc2 Genneia\uc2 , GEMSA, IRSA and MSU Energy were affirmed and the recovery rating were revised to 'RR3' from 'RR4', one notch above their FC IDR.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 Argentina\par \par \uc2 Please refer to each issuers recent Rating Action Commentary for potential developments that may result in a positive rating action.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 Argentina\par \par \uc2 Please refer to each issuers recent Rating Action Commentary for potential developments that may result in a negative rating action.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Non-Financial Corporate issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Issuer Profile\par \par \uc2 See issuers' most recent committee packages for more information.\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\par \par \uc2 The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020221104eib400032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
