\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 A country in black and white\b0\par\par\uc2 4,728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Cristina Kirchner and Silvina Batakis On April 26, 1985, the radicalism in power made a great mobilization to the Plaza de Mayo to listen to its leader, President Ra\u250\'00\'FAl Alfons\u237\'00\'EDn. It was a climate of debate throughout Latin America regarding the adjustments. Peronism was harassing the radical government because it did not resolve to declare the foreign debt unpaid, as Alan Garc\u237\'00\'EDa had done in Peru. That popular mobilization received a kind of cold water bucket from the balcony, because the President declared at that moment a call, which later became legendary, to the war economy. It was the prelude to the Austral Plan. That night, frustrated, the two leaders who had led that mobilization, Enrique Nosiglia and Leopoldo Moreau, locked themselves up with Alfons\u237\'00\'EDn in Olivos and there was a sort of brawl. Probably, it was the time when they were most angry with the President.\par \par \uc2  So much so that, according to those who remember, an aide-de-camp, in charge of the President's security, became nervous. They were frustrated because they were asking for distribution and they were given an adjustment. Something similar, in a much less scenographic way, must be happening today with Kirchnerism, led by Cristina Kirchner. If anyone celebrated the departure of Mart\u237\'00\'EDn Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n as Minister of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenor\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the great powers that ask \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  for adjustment in the name of economic equilibrium", as the clich\u233\'00\'E9 goes, they must now be disenchanted. Why? Because today Silvina Batakis not only ratified the concept, the interpretation of the economic problem defended by Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n, but also threatened with more severe adjustment measures, at least in appearance. We will have to see in the coming weeks if what the Minister of Economy said was nothing more than a rhetoric addressed to the Fund, to the markets that are worried, if we should only expect from her mimicry, or if there is really going to be a more severe adjustment than the one promised by her predecessor. Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n left. Batakis arrived. And the tourniquet seems to tighten a little more. Cristina -we do not know yet- may be like Nosiglia and Moreau. The logic of today, of these announcements, continues to be the logic agreed with the Fund. Here there is no lack of dollars due to trade imbalance problems, in the balance of payments, of dollars going out and coming into the country, as the Vice President thinks. What she calls "external restriction". What we have is an overabundance, an excess, of pesos. And this excess of pesos, as Guzman has explained so many times and as almost the entire community of economic professionals maintains, is due to the fact that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is obliged to issue pesos because it is the only way to finance a bulging deficit. Those excess pesos go to the dollar as a store of value. They seek refuge in the dollar or in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to avoid the loss of purchasing power and the deterioration of wages that is behind this tsunami of pesos that has to do with the BCRA's issuance to finance a deficit that has gotten out of control. This theory, which is the theory on which Guzman's agreement with the Fund is based, was not only subscribed by Batakis, but she also promises more adjustment to shrink the deficit and avoid such issuance. She declares herself to be a "fiscalist" and important spokespersons of the ruling party, such as Representative Eduardo Vald\u233\'00\'E9s, for example, applauded her for being "fiscalist". It remains to be seen if Cristina Kirchner applauds her as well. 11/07/2022 Argentina's Minister of Economy, Silvana Batakis What are Silvina Batakis' measures? How much of them are genuine, cheating or simulation? There are five important measures. The first one: she proposes to reform the financial administration law to expand the capacity of the Minister of Economy in the management of spending in areas that until now were not under her control. Trust funds, decentralized agencies such as the one that governs the life of the airports, known as ORSNA. In other words, a number of entities that were outside the direct fiscal command of the Ministry, now come under the fiscal orbit of Batakis, who wants a monopoly on the management of the treasury. It will have to be seen how much this expansion of the minister's fiscal monitoring over areas of the Government causes political friction, and who are the ones in those offices that, until now, had been safe from Guzman's adjustments. Now they must wait for Batakis' adjustments. It will also be necessary to determine to whom they report and what their political terminals are. Second important measure. The Minister tells all government officials: "I will be authorizing your expenses, the budget execution, what you can spend from the allocated budget, not every quarter, but every month. I will keep you shorter. I will give you as much as I collect". There are those who say, very sophisticated, that maybe it is worse. Why? Because authorizing an expense every three months allowed to liquefy it with inflation. Now, probably, that effect will be nullified because Batakis will have monthly authorizations. If a historian such as Pablo Gerchunoff were watching these measures -and he was surely watching them- he would say "history returns, history returns, history returns in '62, history returns in '72, history returns in '82\u8242\'20\'32", three years in which Jorge Wehbe, who was famous for procrastinating payments, governed the Argentine economy. A supplier would go and ask him for payment because his invoice was overdue and he would say "come later, come next week, come next month. I cash a check and pay you. If I don't cash a check, I'm not going to pay you". That is to say, those who are looking at all these measures today are the suppliers of the State, the contractors of public works. And they should look at them in this sense because you do not have to go to Wehbe, you have to go to Batakis. Because if you look at the management of Batakis as Minister of Economy of Daniel Scioli in the province of Buenos Aires, you discover that she was pedaling expenses and payments. All that configured as a kind of wrinkle that runs forward uy touched Mar\u237\'00\'EDa Eugenia Vidal , who had to take charge with Hern\u225\'00\'E1n Lacunza of payments of invoices issued by Batakis and Scioli. In other words, Wehbe does not return, Batakis returns. Silvina Batakis was part of Daniel Scioli's Cabinet. Third measure. Tax increase. The tax rate does not increase, but the amount of the property on which the tax rate is applied increases. There will be a revaluation of the assets that serve as tax base for the State to collect. What will the opposition say? Nothing. Because that is what Macri did in the city of Buenos Aires. There is a study of the Urbe Foundation, of the deputy of the Buenos Aires Legislature Juan Manuel Vald\u233\'00\'E9s, which focused on the tax problem in the city of Buenos Aires and detected that, if we take the whole period of government of the Pro in relation to the gross product of the City, the tax pressure increased 53%. This means that we are facing a political class that spends too much and that, instead of adjusting expenses, increases taxes. At the time, Cristina Kirchner wanted to charge personal property tax on those valuations adjusted by Macri. And in the Pro said: "It is against the porte\u241\'00\'F1os". A double standard. To charge the real estate tax or the ABL, they charge with a valuation of the property, the closest to the market value. But if the national government wants to charge wealth tax or personal property tax, the previous value is maintained. A double standard of Macrismo to evaluate its own tax policy and that of Kirchnerism. Another measure of Batakis, very important: he ratified something that Guzman did not do, which was in the agreement with the Fund but that he apparently did not want to do. Raising the interest rate above inflation. What is called real interest rate. This means that, if you have pesos in a fixed term, the bank remunerates them more than inflation and you tend to want to have those pesos. This is what you do not want when there is inflation and the real interest rate is negative. Batakis, so that there is no flight to the dollar and people do not preserve the value of their income in the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says that he is going to raise the interest rate in pesos above inflation, which is what Guzman said he was going to do when he committed himself to the IMF. This has two consequences. The first one: those funds are going to withdraw credit to the market. In other words, it is a recessionary measure. There is a second dimension: the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to raise the interest rate of the Leliqs. The debt ball, the quasi-fiscal deficit -as the technicians call it- is going to be even bigger. In the long or short term, we are going to have a problem. Entrance of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Argentina Batakis also ratified the segmentation of tariffs. This was opposed by Cristina Kirchner, through the Undersecretary of Energy, Federico Basualdo. There are two drawbacks. One is conceptual. Kirchnerism does not want to raise tariffs. In fact, it does not want to raise them, but to keep them low. Because in relation to inflation, energy will be worth less. But Cristina Kirchner understands it as a rate increase, she looks at the nominal and not the relation with inflation. Will they admit to Batakis what they did not admit to Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n? The Vice President in her speech said: "We want to talk about politics and not about people". Here the policy is the same. It is the same, corrected and increased. The second problem, which Batakis does not answer, is the unknown that Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n had on his desk: How is the implementation going to be? Is it going to be a form that has to be filled in? If those who deserve the subsidy do not sign it for whatever reason, will they receive the full rate? How many people will it apply to and how many will forget to apply for it? And when the rate hike arrives, what political noise will there be? This enigma that was on Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n's table will be on Batakis' table. Fortunately, Basualdo - in a fight with the former minister for not wanting to increase the tariffs - now says: "I am not summoned to this process because I was excluded by a resolution. But, if they want, I will help. Although I don't have to sign anything. I have nothing to do with what Batakis is going to do". That's a fancy way of saying that he refrains from ruling on whether he likes it or not. In chess, the obvious, the basic principle, is that White moves and then Black reacts. This gives White more power. Those who have power, move. Black reacts. In this universe of the ruling party, this logic is inverted. The black ones move, which is Alberto and his team, and now we are waiting for the white ones, who are more powerful, to answer: Cristina Kirchner and those who surround her. What level of tolerance is there going to be to this reinforced adjustment of Batakis when there was so little tolerance with the adjustment of Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n? This is the main political problem. The same question remains: What political backing does this program have? Guzman ended up telling his friends that his problem was Alberto and not Cristina. Because when Cristina criticizes the government and calls it socially insensitive, she says things that make the government teams bitter. Why? Because they admire her. It is not Cristina talking about Macri's government, it is Cristina talking about a government made up of people who follow her . Therefore, when she spoke against Guzman, officials tended to spend more to disprove what she said because of social insensitivity. And Alberto Fernandez, committing to the program, let it pass. This inconvenience, which is not technical or adjustment level but political support to the Government's program, is what makes many people who are not Kirchnerists -including people acidly critical of Cristina Kirchner- think that the only solution is that Cristina assumes power and that she, with the levers of the Government, turns to the center, as a sort of Lula da Silva, as is happening in Colombia with Gustavo Petro, as is happening in Chile with Gabriel Boric. Of course, there is a difficulty that distinguishes Argentina from those countries. In both Colombia and Chile, there is a huge margin to increase taxes. That is to say, it is possible to seek fiscal balance by increasing taxes, which is the path sought by the fiscalist left. The drama in Argentina is that this path is also exhausted, that is why the adjustment is so costly for politicians. A fiscalist Cristina would be the solution to this problem. Some say: "Even with a change of government, with Cristina in front agitating the people against the adjustment, this has no solution either". It is a thesis to think about. The political problem remains the same: skyrocketing inflation. A graph above, made by economist Fernando Marull shows the current problem, what is coming and especially the political history of the last few years. He takes from 2007 to now and looks at the relationship between inflation and nominal wage. Between mid-2010 and 2014, at the height of Cristina's second term, you see how nominal wages are well above inflation. That could be thought of as a political triumph. Then, it loses in 2015, when inflation exceeds wages. When Macri takes over, inflation exceeds wages, but in 2017 he wins the elections and we see wages above inflation. Then comes an inflationary outbreak in parallel with the 2019 primaries. Inflation falls wages fall in the great quarantine recession and inflation shoots up madly. But wages follow and are even at the moment. What is happening is that economists are forecasting that by the end of the year inflation will be at 90%. The wage bargaining agreements will follow it because in all collective bargaining agreements there is a clause that is triggered when inflation exceeds what was agreed upon. Most probably, as announced today by the Minister of Labor, there will be a reopening of the bargaining process in September in order to recover what has been lost in terms of inflation. What effect will this have on the economy, will all companies be able to pay or will there be difficulties and more 'negreo'? In other words, people will go into the informal sector because they cannot be paid in any other way. This question is also valid for the higher tax pressure of the revaluation. Does this improve tax collection or does it increase the general informality of the economy? Is the State going to be able to follow this line? If you look at page 13 of the last IMF report, it says that public sector salaries should be frozen and pensions should be liquidated. That is what the Fund told Guzman and it must have been repeated to Batakis. That is why the speech she gave on Monday talking to the Fund and to the market. This is only part of the drama. When we look at Marull's chart we are talking about six million people. There is an increasingly important and voluminous universe, which are the informal workers who live on plans or on odd jobs. Those are the most punished because they do not have a trade unionist to defend them, to mobilize. Don't they have one? I ask because Juan Grabois has just said that, in the face of Batakis' adjustment, it is necessary to mobilize the sectors of the popular economy, the vulnerable who are outside this chart. There is a competition between the Kirchnerist, pro-government left and the Trotskyist left. Before Grabois sets a date for this mobilization, the Partido Obrero has already set it: this Thursday. Juan Grabois It is necessary to take a good look at the problem of the informal workers, the pressure of Grabois, who demands a universal salary, and the Polo Obrero (Workers' Pole) on the Government to increase the plans. This overlaps with a political conflict between Kirchnerism and the social movements. The first one to state that there is an enormous discomfort with the political power acquired by the social movements was "El Cuervo" Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Larroque. It is the new Argentina, where in the center are not the unions that represent privileged people, people who are defended by their labor agreement, who receive vacations, retirement and who have social security. Now, at the center of the social claim are the unemployed and the informal workers. They generate the worst conflict for the ruling party because they speak to Kirchnerism and claim in the name of the poor. It is an unthinkable scene for Cristina. They are now mobilized and are at the center of the scene. A question for those who defend the basic salary, which would be a lovely dream. Does it have an effect on inflation? Because that is going to mean emission. If we issue more, is there going to be more inflation and is it going to further degrade the wages of those whose incomes we want to improve? They say no, because issuance does not generate inflation. Then, why a universal basic salary? Why don't they make us all millionaires, why do they fall short, why do they tax us? Why doesn't the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue issuing and we are happy? This is a question for Grabois. We should mobilize to be all millionaires. After all, it doesn't cost anything. Behind this, he said, there is a political conflict. The social movements are not only threatened because their bases are dissatisfied, but they are threatened by Cristina's discourse and the pressure of politics. They make demonstrations of power. This weekend, on July 9, the Evita Movement held a congress to show Cristina who they are and how much they weigh. The message of Emilio P\u233\'00\'E9rsico, the leader of the Evita Movement, together with his "brain", Fernando "El Chino" Navarro -who yesterday made statements in Graciela Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez Meijide's program in favor of Grabois' universal salary- is speaking to Cristina. This is a problem for Alberto Fernandez because this group is almost the last thing he has left as his own power base. But, of course, they pass the bill to him, which in the internal conflict of the Frente de Todos is becoming more and more costly. Among other things, because the bill is for mayoral candidacies against the Kirchnerist mayors in almost all the conurbano. Emilio P\u233\'00\'E9rsico, from Movimiento Evita The problem of inflation is not limited to the salary or the social benefits received by the poorest. There is an exchange rate problem. To the extent that the official dollar is still and inflation increases, the exchange rate starts to lag and this generates a number of additional distortions. This exchange rate tension is probably more visually impacting than the inflation problem. The deterioration of wages is compensated, even if it is for a while, with the wage bargaining process, which keeps pace with inflation. For those who believe that it is only a problem of lack of dollars, it is the cepo, because the movement of pesos to dollars would not represent an anomaly linked to the fiscal problem. It is curious: as a brilliant economist pointed out, when Peronism governs, it is a market coup; and when Macri governs and there is a lack of dollars, it is the flight of the President's friends, who "took it all". We have seen a thousand times more restrictions,announce measures that block more and more access to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. In December 1948, the newspaper La Gaceta announced: "Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be delivered for trips abroad". It was the first Per\u243\'00\'F3n, the one who did not yet need an adjustment, which came only in 52. We have very old problems and we are coming back to the same problem all the time. This flight to the dollar, this pressure with a still dollar that does not assimilate inflation, with a dollar that is the only price that does not move, generates exchange backwardness. This chart above shows the history of the dollar from 2001 onwards. You can see the appreciations and depreciations over time. It should be clarified that this is the real, multilateral exchange rate, it is a comparison of the peso not only with the dollar, but also includes international inflation and all \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with which Argentina trades. Part of the great devaluation of 300% at the end of 2001. We also see the golden era of N\u233\'00\'E9stor Kirchner's government, with a balanced, stable, competitive exchange rate, which allowed for fiscal and trade surpluses. Afterwards, during Cristina's government there was exchange backwardness, i.e. cheap dollar so that we could access things that were expensive. In 2015, you see a devaluation that occurs when Macri with Prat-Gay release the cepo, although in the graph you can see that it is not so big, it is a small peak of devaluation. Many believe that the following was Macri's big problem, and it makes you want to believe them: the exchange rate backwardness of the Sturzenegger era at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which generated a demand for dollars by the economy that was unleashed in a crisis when the international markets said 'the U.S. interest rate goes up, everyone towards the dollar'. They said: "we have to flee Argentina because the Argentines, with such a cheap dollar, live beyond their means and need a lot of dollars". Then we have the whole Macri's devaluation and, again, the exchange backwardness -which many judge as demagogic- of the current Government. One of the behaviors that is producing this exchange rate lag that does not pick up inflation has to do with the sale of soybeans. We are at a historical low. Farmers have never withheld so much product as now, to the point that large grain companies have put their processing plants in maintenance because there is no product, at a time when they should be working at full capacity. The farmer understands that the soybeans he has in the silo-bag are dollars. As there is a large exchange rate delay, he suspects that at some point the price will have to be updated. So he thinks: "I'm not selling now, I'm waiting for the devaluation". By waiting for it, he delivers fewer dollars, sells less and induces it. The market always works as a self-fulfilling prophecy. What do I do with soybeans? I keep it, but a little bit goes to "buy dollars". They buy fertilizers, seeds, things that are denominated in dollars, taking advantage of the fact that the dollar is cheap. That is to say, I ask for more dollars, I consume more dollars and I also deliver fewer dollars because I do not sell. This is the behavior of a country without \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Of a country that is permanently waiting for a devaluation because its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is falling apart, is being destroyed. I am going to put it in Cristina's terms. If she understood this she would say the following: "It is a country that lost its monetary sovereignty". A contrary case: I am talking about the parity of the Bolivian peso with the dollar. Bolivia had a devaluation at the time of the great international crisis in 2008 and from that moment until now an era of stability. That is to say, Bolivians have a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that if we compare it with the Argentinean peso, it is a strong and stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So much so that, in the north of Argentina, many people save in Bolivian pesos, not in dollars. They see them as a desirable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We are talking about the government of Luis Arce, Bolivarian if you will, but with fiscal discipline. This is the tradition of Bolivia's balanced economic policy and the Argentines in the north feel like having Bolivian pesos. The same happens on the border with Paraguay and the Guarani. The same happens with Uruguay, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not only maintained, but also appreciates, becoming stronger against the dollar. These distortions produce behaviors, not only in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Other conducts. Such as the smuggling seen in La Quiaca. This is the deterioration of the Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the smuggling in the whole border, because it also happens with Brazil and Bolivia. Another problem we have always been talking about is the pressure on the dollar due to the cost of energy. International prices are also skyrocketing due to Russia's attack on Ukraine. That is why Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n wanted to negotiate with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  that energy prices -what we spend on fuel and gas purchases- should not go over the fiscal deficit line. The Fund says: "But adjust something, because they did not comply with any item of the program". This is what the market is seeing, to whom Batakis spoke, which are the banks, the insurance companies, the financial institutions that have securities in pesos. Because not only people are already repudiating the peso but also the securities in pesos. There is a maturity of 500,000 million pesos at the end of this month and Batakis has to renew that placement. Moreover, he has to extend it. It is not enough to renew the 500,000 million pesos. He needs more than that. The technicians, Miguel Kiguel, for example, say: "Well, half of those bonds are held by the State, so they are going to be renewed". To some extent, Batakis wants to expand the power of the Minister of Economy over all the State entities in order to be able to manage debt as well. There is 50% left, which is held by the market. The 25% can be renegotiated amicably. There is 25% left over which we do not know what is going to happen. So that these securities are not destroyed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys them and issues more: more Leliqs, more rates, more snowball. Batakis does not have to renew 100%. He needs 140% because he has to cover more deficit. This is the problem Batakis is in right now and he is telling the market: "Lend me because I am going to be more austere, because I am going to freeze vacancies, because I am going to have more control over spending, over more public offices, because I am going to really segment and abolish subsidies, besides raising the interest rate". He is at a crossroads similar to the one Domingo Cavallo was at in August 2001, when he received the hot inheritance of convertibility. With an extraordinary difference; Cavallo could not issue. Batakis and Pesce can, even if such issuance has negative consequences. In August 2001, Cavallo sat down with the banks, with those who lent to them, to make a big renegotiation of that debt. A fortnight before, more or less, Clar\u237\'00\'EDn published this cover: "The State limits its expenses: it will only pay what it collects". This is Batakis going to a debt problem. To recapitulate, Alfons\u237\'00\'EDn in 1985 with a war economy, Per\u243\'00\'F3n in 1948, Wehbe in 1962, 72 and 82, Cavallo in 01, Batakis today, we are eternally going back to the same thing. It is the myth of Sisyphus, that character of Greek mythology who was condemned for life to climb a stone up a mountain and, when he reached the top, it slipped from his hands and he had to climb it again as an eternal punishment. A country that reads the newspapers of 2001 or '48 and realizes that they are today's newspapers. A country in black and white.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220712ei7c005i2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
