\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Batakis will aggravate the crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 1,153 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's moral and institutional corrosion and its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  and expropriationist approach has built a very bad local and international reputation for incentives to savings, investment and productivity.\par \par \uc2 The current government has not only done nothing to revert this situation, but it has dedicated itself to deepen the problem by making Argentina an absolutely refractory place for capital. For this reason, after the untimely resignation of \uc2 Mart\u237\'00\'EDn Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n\uc2 , the name of his replacement in the Ministry of Economy was never relevant since, with this level of discredit and credibility, that decision would only determine the speed at which the country will enter a crisis that we have not even begun to suffer in its true magnitude.\par \par \uc2 After her swearing in as new Minister of Economy and after a telegraphic and empty message, Silvina Batakis, gradually gave notes in different media to refer in greater depth to the measures that will govern during her term of office. After listening to her, she leaves enormous concerns because, far from correcting bad diagnoses and promoting the transit towards the resolution of the serious problems that afflict us, she rides on the same delirium of the government, its fight with reality, the story of denial and a brutal load of socialist sophisms that have been refuted decades ago . DIRIGISTIC VISION For those who did not know her, Batakis offered an undoubtedly dirigiste vision. She argued that, ``since resources are scarce, the role of the state is to define where those resources are allocated and why they are allocated in a certain way''.\par \par \uc2 He also praised political activity as a transforming instrument that allows the fulfillment of citizens' dreams. In this way and with the immoral pretension of disposing of the fruits of other people's labor, he pretends to legitimize his function and that of the rest of the bureaucrats who have dedicated themselves to expand an unaffordable government and systematically push people to misery and indigence.\par \par \uc2 That sidereal distance that separates reality from the ideas that the new head of the economy portfolio subscribes, does not allow her to see that the real dream of the people is that the State and all the parasitic gangsters let them live in peace.\par \par \uc2 Batakis showed the most mediocre face of collectivism when she pretended to demonstrate the importance of the role of the State by saying that, during the quarantine, the government arbitrated all the means for Argentines to have an ATP (Emergency Assistance to Work). They locked us all in a totalitarian quarantine, broke our businesses and assisted us with our own taxes. Poverty at its highest level.\par \par \uc2 He also insisted on the multi-causality of inflation -among them, the false concept of inflation of expectations and speculation-. Not understanding that inflation is exclusively the exogenous expansion of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will lead the minister to undertake quixotic attacks against price makers, to promote agreements towards "reasonable price baskets" and other repeated failed attempts. THE DOLLAR The economist described the free dollar as marginal and downplayed its importance. On the other hand, she said she was satisfied with the reference offered by the so-called official dollar because of its competitiveness and because it concentrates the largest volume of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, nobody loses sight of the fact that the official dollar is not a price as such. A price is a transfer of property rights and it is determined by the market according to its changing valuations, therefore, there is no price without property rights, there is no price without free markets. The official dollar is, no more and no less, what has been called maximum price, terms over which no holder of that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is willing to operate. Only the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the only and obligatory window for foreign trade, embarks on this delirium of shearing exporters and releasing foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in dribs and drabs for the liquidation of imports by means of maddening restrictions.\par \par \uc2 A common mistake in our environment is to fall into the mercantilist fallacy of the supposed advantages of a positive trade balance, that is, exporting more than what is imported. As Alberto Benegas Lynch (Jr.) has said, it would be wonderful if, with the proceeds from the export of a pin, all the goods and services existing in the world could be imported. Fr\u233\'00\'E9d\u233\'00\'E9ric Bastiat illustrates the point with the story of a French merchant who owns wines worth 10 and exports them to England selling them for 20. With the 20 obtained, he buys cotton in England to finally import it to his country.\par \par \uc2 These movements, for many economists, represent an unfavorable balance of trade. Completing his example, the author invites us to imagine that the same French merchant registers the departure of the same wines at a value of 10 but, in this case, manages to sell them in England at only 5 due to breakages suffered by the shipment during the trip. With the 5 obtained from the failed sale of the wines, he buys cotton in England and also imports it to France. Even in the context of an absolute loss, the so-called modern economy assumes these last foreign trade \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a favorable trade balance. TRAVEL The Minister also referred to the dollars required for foreign travel. She pointed out that the provision of U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for tourism sacrifices productivity and that ``the right to travel collides with the right to generate jobs''. Those who insist on dirigisme find themselves trapped in the need to impose a growing and infernal labyrinth of regulations to cover the sun with their hand.\par \par \uc2 In another passage of one of the interviews, he justified the Universal Basic Wage shortly before saying that ''Argentina is the fastest growing economy in the world,'' which left many perplexed. He referred to this as a valid tool to assist from the State those who cannot get a job when Argentina becomes part of the Revolution 4.0. With this he adheres to the mistaken theory of technological unemployment, which loses sight of the fact that work is always a scarce factor due to the fact that human nature and imperfection condition us to unlimited needs. On the other hand, if the same minister acknowledges having been the victim of a cable theft in her neighborhood that left her without fixed telephony and wifi, I think we are closer to the pre-industrial misery of the early eighteenth century than to the Revolution 4.0.\par \par \uc2 Nothing good awaits us if central planning and committees of enlightened bureaucrats are insisted upon. These recipes only sacrifice individual liberties and the rights for which our forefathers have given their lives. The only way out is to assume the costs of having pretended to live in Jauja and drastically reduce the state apparatus, an infernal machinery that not only consumes dreams but also leads us to live progressively miserable lives.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220710ei79001sb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
