\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 How money printing destroyed Argentina and may destroy others\b0\par\par\uc2 1,138 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The most dangerous words in monetary policy and economics are "this time it is different". The big mistake of Argentine politicians is to believe that inflation is multi-causal and that everything is solved with increasing doses of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionism\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The consumer price index in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  experienced a year-on-year rise of 58% in April 2022, which is 2.9 percentage points higher than the variation recorded last March. A real catastrophe. Inflation in Argentina is more than six times higher than in Uruguay, five times higher than in Chile and four times higher than in Brazil and Paraguay, neighboring countries exposed to the same global problems.\par \par \uc2 Read also: Argentina: two wars against inflationRead also: Inflation in Argentina: the highest in 30 yearsNo, inflation in Argentina is not multi-causal, it has only one cause: an extractive and confiscatory monetary policy. Printing of pesos without control and without demand. Argentina inflates its monetary base to finance excessive, inflated and destructive public spending.\par \par \uc2 ADVERTISEMENTSo farthis year, the monetary base has increased by 43.83%, which is truly insane. Price inflation is 58.2%.\par \par \uc2 In the last three years, the monetary base has increased 179.73 % and in ten years more than 1,543.8 %. This is an economic aberration, not an "inclusive monetary policy", as described by Axel Kicillof, governor of Buenos Aires.\par \par \uc2 In the last ten years, the Argentine peso has lost 99% of its value against the dollar. It is an expropriation of the country's wealth by printing useless pesos.\par \par \uc2 Many Argentine Peronists say that the United States also massively increases its money supply and has no inflation. The argument does not hold water. The U.S. monetary base is growing at a rate of 9.9%, six times less than Argentina's, and, moreover, the U.S. also suffers from 8.5% inflation. At the height of the money glut in the United States, the monetary base grew by 26.9%. In the same period, Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  base tripled, with a decreasing demand for pesos, while the global and local demand for US dollars grew.\par \par \uc2 In aggregate terms, the money supply, including all money in circulation, has soared in Argentina by 2,328.09 % in ten years, while in the United States it has doubled. In other words, the aggregate money supply in Argentina in the last decade has increased at a rate more than eleven times that of the United States. Only Venezuela has carried out such folly.\par \par \uc2 It is not only foreigners do not demand pesos nor do they accept them in international transactions, which is a reality. The citizens of Argentina do not accept their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a store of value, unit of measure and method of payment most of the time.\par \par \uc2 The saddest thing is that many say that Argentina was dollarized before and it did not work. In Argentina there was no dollarization: there was a deception in which it was declared that one peso was equal to one dollar. Like the stablecoins crashing in the market today, the so-called dollarization was simply a fallacy, and when the bubble burst, policy makers continued to further destroy the purchasing power of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The United States does not have this problem... yet. Confidence in the U.S. dollar is not falling yet; it is rising, and so it is strengthening globally relative to most of the world's major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The main reason for this relative strength is that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  monitors global demand for US dollars and is seen as taking decisive action against inflation. However, the oft-repeated fallacy that massive money printing does not cause inflation ended abruptly with the disaster committed in 2020. The United States, the eurozone and most of the world's economies decided to meet a supply shock with massive demand-side policies, financing the unprecedented increase in government spending with newly created money, and inflation shot up vigorously.\par \par \uc2 ADVERTISEMENTTheU.S. dollar does not suffer because the alternatives are worse. Other countries print their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even more aggressively or also have capital controls and lack legal and investor certainty. However, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  should not rest on its laurels. Confidence in a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a store of value, unit of measure and means of payment can disappear faster than policymakers imagine. The current system of checks and balances in the U.S. economy and the open financial system maintain the dollar as the world's reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the storm clouds are gathering. On the one hand, US policymakers are increasingly advocating even more aggressive monetary policies to finance an unaffordable and growing public budget. On the other hand, some countries are beginning to look for alternatives to the dollar to sell commodities. These are still distant threats, but they should not be ignored.\par \par \uc2 The reader may think that Argentina is a far-fetched example to compare with the United States, but the exaggeration is deliberate. Just look at the history of governments pushing massive budget increases and financing them with an increasingly unpopular \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the risks to the euro or the dollar become more obvious. The reader may say that citizens of developed economies would never allow something like this to happen in their nations, but Argentina was also a rich and prosperous economy decades ago. It was one of the richest and largest economies in the world at the beginning of the 20th century. A combination of protectionism, populist \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies and crazy monetary decisions destroyed the economy, and it has never recovered.\par \par \uc2 All the aforementioned insane decisions of the Argentine governments are now defended by politicians all over the United States and Europe: "It won't happen to us" and "This time it's different". It is not different.\par \par \uc2 Empires always fall because they begin to destroy the purchasing power of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and because their position in the world collapses as protectionism and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionism\b0 \highlight\uc2  erode confidence in government and its institutions. Once the destruction begins, it is only a matter of time before citizens start saving in gold or other real reserves of value. There is a lesson for all those who advocate constantly pushing the limits of monetary policy and isolationist measures. Once pushed too far, there is no turning back.\par \par \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  used to say that gold is money and everything else is credit. Credit is confidence. Once confidence is lost, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dissolves. This is a lesson for everyone.\par \par \uc2 This article was originally published on Mises.orgDaniel\par \par \uc2 Lacalle\par \par \uc2 , PhD, economist and fund manager, is the author of the bestselling books Freedom or Equality (2020), Escape from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trap (2017), The Energy World Is Flat (2015) and Life in the Financial Markets (2014) . Tags: Axel KicillofMoney printinginflationinterventionism\par \par \uc2