\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 How will sanctions against Russia for the invasion of Ukraine affect Argentina?\b0\par\par\uc2 3,229 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The sanctions imposed on Russia by European countries, the United States and Canada in retaliation for the military attack on Ukraine are aimed at forcing \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to back down from his escalation of war against the former Soviet republic of Ukraine. However, its effects will far exceed the Russian economy and will be felt around the world, through higher prices of agricultural and energy commodities - mainly wheat and oil - higher inflation and food shortages.\par \par \uc2 Beyond the good harmony that \uc2 Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\uc2  sought to have with Putin in his visit last February, before the outbreak of the war conflict, and the fact that the Argentine Government announced that it will not apply sanctions against Moscow for the invasion of Ukraine, our country will not be exempt from receiving the blows of the measures imposed by the main Western nations. The President's offer to his Russian counterpart to make Argentina "the gateway to Latin America" will not prevent Argentina from suffering the first local impacts.\par \par \uc2 Although the commercial exchange is low in relation to that with other countries, there are specific sectors that are already being affected. Because of a combination of decisions that have been implemented this last week and that, inevitably, have repercussions in Argentina, according to the different specialists and representatives of different sectors consulted by Infobae.\par \par \uc2 They range from the decision of the maritime companies not to arrive at the Russian ports to unload the goods, to the air blockade that also prevents the entry of products and documentation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  necessary for the Argentine suppliers to be able to carry out the import. In addition to this, there is the uncertainty about the goods in transit and the additional costs of being stranded and, mainly, the decision of Western countries to exclude Russia from the SWIFT system (\uc2 Society for Worldwide Interbank Financial Telecommunication\uc2  ), the high security network that facilitates payments between 11,000 financial institutions in 200 countries, and which allows transactions of billions of dollars around the world.\par \par \uc2 Argentine sales to Russia\par \par \uc2 In 2021, our country exported to the Russian nation agricultural products for USD 680 million, according to official data from the National Institute of Statistics and Census (INDEC). Citrus fruits, such as lemons and oranges; pears and apples from Patagonia; peanuts and dried plums; meat to make sausages; dairy products; and fish from the South Atlantic, such as prawns, were leaving from Argentine ports to that destination until now.\par \par \uc2 "Our exports to Russia are less than 1% of the total, which is about USD 75 billion. It is not a very relevant volume. As a comparison, we sell to the United States for USD 4.5 billion and to China for USD 8 billion. But for those who participate in these exports, the sanctions against Russia are a problem. It is never good to lose anything in the international market", said Marcelo Elizondo, former executive director of Fundaci\u243\'00\'F3n Exportar and current director of the consulting firm DNI, in an interview with Infobae.\par \par \uc2 "But indirectly, the increase in energy commodities will increase worldwide. Argentina imported gas at USD 8 per BTU and it is estimated that this year it will be between USD 25 and 30 per BTU, which is what it will have to pay. Bolivia will produce less this year and it was cheaper for us to bring it from Bolivia through a gas pipeline than to go out and buy it in a ship that costs three times more expensive".\par \par \uc2 No sea freight\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday of last week, all maritime companies suspended their services to Russian ports, which blocked the arrival of goods by that route. This decision of the shipping companies has major implications for the sanctioned country itself, but also for world trade and Argentina. "Local producers who used to sell to Russia will have to look for other markets, which is not easy because they are mostly traditional markets", warned Gustavo Id\u237\'00\'EDgoras, president of the Chamber of the Oil Industry and Grain Exporters Center (Ciara-CEC ).\par \par \uc2 The suspension of maritime freights to Russia and Ukraine also affects the entire \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of bilateral trade. "There is concern for the goods in transit, both for those that arrived before the measure came into force and were stranded at the port, and for those that have already left and the 30% advance payment was collected, or the totality of the cargo, but have not yet reached the hands of the Russian customer. A ship from Buenos Aires to Russia takes 35 days. So, there is a large number of containers that were affected by this decision of the shipping companies", explained Mat\u237\'00\'EDas Garc\u237\'00\'EDa Tu\u241\'00\'F1\u243\'00\'F3n, coordinator of the Argentine-Russian Chamber of Commerce and Industry (CACIAR), a civil association that groups private Argentine companies aimed at promoting foreign trade with Russia, without any link with the government of that country.\par \par \uc2 "The usual destination ports for our exports to Russia are Rotterdam (in the Netherlands) or Antwerp (Belgium), and from there they are transshipped. That is the section that can no longer be done since Wednesday. So we have to find another destination for the cargo that arrived or is about to arrive in the next few days. And for each day the goods are stopped at the port, you have to pay, plus, for example, if it requires cold. For this reason, the Argentine-Russian Chamber of Commerce is suggesting today that all \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  be put on stand-by (suspense)", Garc\u237\'00\'EDa Tu\u241\'00\'F1\u243\'00\'F3n told Infobae.\par \par \uc2 One of the sectors already affected is peanuts. "In 2021, only to Russia, there were 40,000 tons for USD 52 million (FOB value). It was 60% percent more than in 2020, when we sold 25,000 tons for USD 31 million. As of yesterday (Wednesday), we will no longer be able to load our peanuts destined for the port of St. Petersburg in Russia, and we do not know what will happen to the containers already loaded, where they will go, if they will be diverted to the \uc2 port of Rotterdam\uc2 , where 40% of Argentine peanuts destined for Europe go. But we have just been informed, for example, that there is a maritime congestion in that port and a ship could not unload there", said Diego Yabes, vice-president of the Argentine Peanut Chamber, in an interview with Infobae.\par \par \uc2 "On the other hand, the accumulation of merchandise generates an unforeseen volume, with an oversupply and the consequent fall in the international price. For Brazil - our direct competitor in terms of price, since it does not have any taxes - only Russia and Ukraine represent 50% of its peanut exports", he added.\par \par \uc2 Added to this is the devaluation of the ruble. "The Russian importer will ask to renegotiate the price with the Argentine seller due to the fall of the ruble. Otherwise, he will end up losing money when he sells to his domestic buyer in Russia", warned Garc\u237\'00\'EDa Tu\u241\'00\'F1\u243\'00\'F3n.\par \par \uc2 Two other agricultural products are also feeling the impact of the sanctions: wheat and sunflower oil, in which Ukraine and Russia are Argentina's competitors. In wheat, these two countries contribute 28% of world production. "Russia may disappear from the market due to the sanctions and Ukraine, stopped being able to sell its wheat because the Black Sea ports are not \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This is already causing a rise in international wheat prices. However, Argentina will not be able to take advantage of this situation because the Government does not authorize more exports. Since the end of last year we have had an export ceiling of 14.5 million for this year. But we have already exported 14 million wheat," Id\u237\'00\'EDgoras explained.\par \par \uc2 "The government put this maximum on the volumes to be exported to guarantee that the rest of the wheat goes to the domestic market. But in these days the international price went up, and therefore the domestic market price went up. Today there are 7.5 million tons unsold by Argentine producers", added the head of the Chamber of the Oil Industry and Grain Exporters Center. Precisely because of this situation of historical rise of the grain, which reached almost USD 500 per ton, the Government yesterday made official the creation of a wheat and corn trust fund to "ensure a stable price" of some products. The measure was received with criticism from the different sectors linked to the production and commercialization of grains.\par \par \uc2 In the case of sunflower oil, Ukraine and Russia account for 78% of world trade. "Ukraine in recent years displaced Argentina in sales. It became an agricultural power based on agro-industrial development. That is why it has a lot of foreign direct investment. Between Ukraine and Russia there are 10 million tons of sunflower oil that cannot leave the Black Sea because all ports are closed, and that cannot reach the \uc2 European Union\uc2  , China and India", Id\u237\'00\'EDgoras pointed out.\par \par \uc2 Can Argentina capitalize on this impossibility of locating its sunflower oil that Russia and Ukraine have today, asked Infobae. "No, because we have a small volume to export. We produce 3,100,000 tons a year, and we have a trust to protect oil consumption, and we only export 700,000 tons a year. The rest is for domestic consumption. Due to the government's intention to decouple prices, local consumption is being paid by exporters, who pay domestic producers the difference. In other words, due to the restrictions we have, we cannot take advantage of the possibility of selling more sunflower oil or wheat abroad in view of this new world scenario".\par \par \uc2 In addition to the decision of the shipping companies to suspend maritime charters to Russia, Russia has closed its air space to 36 countries, among them all the main European and Western countries, in response to the sanctions. "This implies that the planes of the main airlines no longer fly to Moscow or St. Petersburg, and that shipments do not arrive, or become more expensive as they have to look for alternative routes, via Asia, with a greater number of stopovers," observed Yabes.\par \par \uc2 Garc\u237\'00\'EDa Tu\u241\'00\'F1\u243\'00\'F3n added that "there are perishable goods, smaller cargoes, samples, or for example vaccines that must be transported by plane because they cannot take 35 days to arrive. The same applies to the documentation for each shipment to Russia, which is sent to the customer by plane so that it can be collected after it is dispatched".\par \par \uc2 What we buy from Russia\par \par \uc2 As regards purchases from Russia, last year they amounted to USD 650 million out of a total of around USD 63 billion that Argentina imports as a whole. That figure far exceeds imports in the years of \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  and the first year of \uc2 Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\uc2  . The reason? The purchase of Sputnik V doses - the first inoculant against COVID 19 to be administered in the country at the beginning of the vaccination campaign, but not yet approved by the European and North American authorities - for USD 223 million. It was the main purchase from Putin's government last year. It is equivalent to one third of the total purchased from Russia.\par \par \uc2 Beyond vaccines against coronavirus, historically we bought inputs for fertilizers (mainly phosphates) and diesel oil. "Now Argentina will have to look for alternative fertilizer suppliers, such as China and Morocco, but it is going to impact the cost. Russia is a very important supplier and the price of raw materials will rise," warned Id\u237\'00\'EDgoras.\par \par \uc2 A shot to the heart of the Russian financial system\par \par \uc2 All those consulted agreed that the measure with the greatest economic impact for Russia is the suspension of seven Russian banks from access to the SWIFT system, preventing financial transactions with buyers and sellers of commodities in that country. The measures crippled the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's assets and froze its transactions, making it impossible to liquidate its assets and thus facilitating a sharp devaluation of the Russian ruble.\par \par \uc2 "Bilaterally, what affects the most is the suspension of SWIFT. The fact that many of the Russian banks have been left out is an issue that Argentina cannot reverse and will suffer the impact immediately in the short term. SWIFT is the one that allows the collection of transactions, and guarantees the information of who is the one who collects or pays. Russia could triangulate with China, but it would not last long, because the traceability of payments in the world is known. From now on it will be complicated to buy from and sell to Russia, even if we are not a relevant customer or supplier in terms of volume. The bulk of Russia's trade is with the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and Asia", warned Elizondo.\par \par \uc2 For Ricardo Lagorio, former ambassador to Russia during the Cambiemos administration and current secretary general of the Argentine Council for International Relations (CARI), "this sanction is the most critical because it practically implies disconnecting a country from all financial flows, they take the blood out of it. It is a very harsh and very intelligent measure. It reaches the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  , and the state-owned banks that can provide financing. Now, any pre-financing for projects and contracts with Russia will be vetoed."\par \par \uc2 This exclusion from the system opens a big question mark over what will happen to payments for exports already made, which had not yet reached the customer in Russia, and unpaid balances. The SWIFT exclusion of seven banks would affect 70% of Russian domestic transactions. "We do not yet know what the consequences of this measure may be, and how it may affect the payment capacity of Russian companies that are our customers, if they cannot pay because they do not have access to pre-financing, or because they do not make bank transactions to make payments. And there will also be companies that are our clients that enter into crisis and are founded because of the war conflict and the sanctions," said Yabes, from the Argentine Peanut Chamber.\par \par \uc2 "The problem is that all transfers are made in dollars and have to go through the United States. There are also \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are made on term, that is, shipments of goods that were agreed to be collected between 30 and 90 days, without knowing that the invasion of Ukraine was going to happen. And, for example, in the export of dairy products to Russia, the five main Argentine companies account for 30% of sales and the first 20, 50%. They will surely be able to find their way around. But the exporter from the provinces who is owed USD 500,000, if he cannot collect them due to the exclusion of the main Russian banks from the SWIFT system, will go bankrupt", warned Garc\u237\'00\'EDa Tu\u241\'00\'F1\u243\'00\'F3n.\par \par \uc2 In addition to this, there is a 180-day limit to liquidate the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that Argentine exporters receive at the official exchange rate. "If you are on day 179 because you gave the Russian client a deadline to pay you, you get into trouble with the AFIP", added this specialist in trade with the Russian Federation for more than 15 years.\par \par \uc2 However, not all Russian banks are affected by this SWIFT suspension. This decision of the European countries to carry out a selective exclusion was criticized because it preserved those entities for which Europe pays for the gas it receives from Russia, such as \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  and \uc2 Gazprombank\uc2  , the two main ones involved in energy sales \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Russia is the world's leading gas exporter and 40% of Europe's gas consumption depends on gas supplied by that country. "It is very difficult to replace. It's not that if they don't buy from Putin, they can buy it from someone else. In time, if this is prolonged, they will end up affecting the gas business", Elizondo analyzed in a dialogue with Infobae.\par \par \uc2 The commercial relationship since Kirchnerism\par \par \uc2 The commercial relationship with Russia since Kirchnerism until today has had its ups and downs. Infobae's Data Unit compared the volumes in dollars of imports and exports from 2003 to date. From this analysis it emerges that the exchange increased during \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  's administration and decreased during \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  's administration. But this is not only due to a question of Kirchnerism's political affinity with Putin. "Macri had lower international prices. It is not only the amount of dollars that entered or left. \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  , from 2012 to 2014, had very high international prices and, perhaps selling less, they implied the same amount of dollars to the country", pointed out the head of the DNI consultancy.\par \par \uc2 From the analysis of the trade balance - the difference between exports and imports from Russia - it emerges that the most deficit years, in which Argentina bought more from Russia than it sold, were those of the second government of the current vice president, exactly between 2011 and 2015. The peak was reached in 2013 when the balance was favorable to Russia by almost USD 1 billion mainly due to Argentina's purchase of gas oil, gas and hydrocarbons.\par \par \uc2 The best year in terms of trade for Argentina was during \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  's first government, in 2009, when sales to Russia exceeded imports from that country by USD 715 million.\par \par \uc2 According to the coordinator of the Argentine-Russian Chamber, "when there is greater State \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the trade balance becomes a deficit for \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When there is a greater State-State connection, where the interest goes elsewhere, Russia manages to sell more to us than we do. When \uc2 Alberto Fernandez\uc2  met with Putin in February, he did not bring anyone from the private sector. He went with many ministers and governors, and there was no businessman or meat producer, for example, who told him that there are plants that have been waiting for two years for Russia to enable them".\par \par \uc2 During Macri's government, the trade balance always had a positive balance, although with fluctuations, and a peak in favor of the balance of USD 410 in the last year of his mandate.\par \par \uc2 Last year, when imports from Russia amounted to USD 650 million - with a third explained only by the purchase of vaccines -, and exports from our country were USD 680, the trade balance was in surplus by USD 29 million. Exports in 2021 - 11% more than in 2010 - were in line with the average of the last 10 years of USD 638 million. By contrast, in 2020, purchases from Russia had been substantially lower: only USD 166 million, against USD 612 million in exports to that country.\par \par \uc2 How the information was processed\par \par \uc2 For the analysis of the numerical information, Infobae's Data Unit accessed INDEC's foreign trade database. Exports and imports from 2003 to 2021 were analyzed, as well as the trade balance between Argentina and Russia in that period.\par \par \uc2 If you wish to visualize or download the spreadsheet with its respective tabs, follow this link.\par \par \uc2 Viewing: Andres Snitcofsky\par \par \uc2 Data upload: Desir\u233\'00\'E9 Santander\par \par \uc2 CONTINUE READING:\par \par \uc2 Eduardo "Wado" de Pedro: "The agreement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  is the beginning of the solution, it avoids a catastrophe in the immediate term".\par \par \uc2 Agreement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  : tensions with La C\u225\'00\'E1mpora accelerate the construction of albertismo to push for the President's reelection\par \par \uc2 Infobae in Kiev: the children's hospital is still working underground, and itsdirector makes a desperate plea to the world\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220306ei36001f3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
