\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Argentina to get US$9.8 billion from \uc2 IMF\uc2  after agreement approval\b0\par\par\uc2 801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Argentina\u8217\'20\'19s government published on Friday the detailed plan for reducing its fiscal deficit and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  financing as part of its pending US$45-billion deal with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The agreement with \uc2 IMF\uc2  staff, announced Thursday, has now been submitted to Argentina\u8217\'20\'19s Congress and will go before a vote sometime in the coming weeks. The country will receive seven billion (US$9.8 billion) of the \uc2 IMF\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s special drawing rights upon approval by lawmakers and the executive board of the Washington-based organisation, according to the document.\par \par \uc2 The memorandum, which comes after two years of negotiations between President \uc2 Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s economic team and \uc2 IMF\uc2  officials, provides the fine-print detail on economic policy and projections that lawmakers have been waiting for before deciding on their vote. Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine is increasing uncertainty around the plan\u8217\'20\'19s baseline assumptions and may force policies to be \u8220\'20\'1Crecalibrated\u8221\'20\'1D as necessary, it said.\par \par \uc2 Argentina is seeking to close its 22nd programme with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  since 1958 before a key payment for about US$2.8 billion comes due in late March. The agreement is expected to face tough criticism from radical-left members of the ruling coalition backed by vice-president \uc2 Cristina Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez de Kirchner\uc2  and would only head to the \uc2 IMF\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s board for final approval if Congress passes it.\par \par \uc2 Argentina\u8217\'20\'19s US$16 billion in bonds due in 2030 slipped 0.7 cent to around 31 cents on the dollar, while securities maturing in 2046 slid about a cent to 28 cents on the dollar, as of 12.30pm in New York.\par \par \uc2 Some key points in the documents:\par \par \uc2 Disbursements\par \par \uc2 If the agreement is passed by congress and the \uc2 IMF\uc2  executive board, Argentina would receive seven billion (US$9.8 billion) of the \uc2 IMF\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s special drawing rights upon approval. It would receive smaller amounts if it passes the quarterly revisions of the by \uc2 IMF\uc2  staff, increasing the total of the programme to US$45 billion.\par \par \uc2 Monetary Policy The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to boost its foreign reserves by at least US$5.8 billion this year and to gradually eliminate money printing to finance government spending, curbing it to zero by 2024. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s dwindling reserves have been a key investor concern, while economists warned that large-scale money printing fuelled higher inflation.\par \par \uc2 The institution, which raised its benchmark rate twice in 2022 ahead of the agreement, will also look to have real monetary policy rates.\par \par \uc2 The memo says it plans to simplify its existing reserve requirements system \u8211\'20\'13 banks\u8217\'20\'19 money held by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to back deposits \u8211\'20\'13 for smaller entities as part of a goal to boost the transmission of its monetary policy. Argentina will also consider consider gradually easing the floor on deposit rates and the ceiling on lending rates for commercial banks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven if the programme does address Argentina\u8217\'20\'19s deficit monetisation, the agreement seems very generous for the country,\u8221\'20\'1D said Alejo Costa, chief Argentina strategist at \uc2 BTG Pactual\uc2  in Buenos Aires. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s not a good thing, as it could give Kirchnerism the space to continue with unorthodox policies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Energy\par \par \uc2 In the energy sector, the government will focus on cutting its expensive subsidies bill, with an emphasis on segmenting households. That means the richest 10 percent would pay full fees for power and natural gas, with subsidies kicking in for poorer families. The savings from peeling back energy subsidies may not be as large as forecast \u8211\'20\'13 0.6 percent of GDP this year \u8211\'20\'13 as global energy prices soar amid the Russian invasion of Ukraine, the document says.\par \par \uc2 The government will also spur investments in energy production and transportation to reduce expensive fuel imports, including by keeping energy bills in line over time with wholesale power and natural gas prices.\par \par \uc2 On climate, the government recommitted to existing announcements for electric-vehicle legislation and a \u8220\'20\'1Cgreen\u8221\'20\'1D economy plan that includes supporting the hydrogen industry.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with the goal of boosting its reserves, according to the memo. However, the government also has a plan to gradually relax those controls over time, though more specific timing and implementation wasn\u8217\'20\'19t provided.\par \par \uc2 Officials intend to maintain the real effective exchange rate of the peso this year at the same levels seen in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Economic outlook Argentina\u8217\'20\'19s economy is expected to grow 3.5 to 4 percent this year, with annual inflation landing in the range of 38 to 48 percent, according to the document. Growth is expected to slow in the coming years as price increases cool too.\par \par \uc2 The government intends to spend over two percent of GDP on infrastructure over the course of the programme.\par \par \uc2 by Patrick Gillespie, \uc2 Bloomberg\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220304ei34009rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
