\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Former key \uc2 IMF\uc2  official says agreement will not lower inflation or boost Argentina's economic growth\b0\par\par\uc2 1,934 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The former regional auditor of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) \uc2 Alejandro Werner\uc2  affirmed that the Government had an intelligent strategy to negotiate with the multilateral organization, although he affirmed that with the new program, inflation will not give way and economic growth will not be more robust.\par \par \uc2 In an interview from Washington, the Mexican economist, who audited the country until last year, told Infobae that it is better for both the \uc2 IMF\uc2  and the government to sign a new program, even if it is not ambitious in terms of reforms.\par \par \uc2 He also relativized the importance of U.S. political influence on the technical profile of the new agreement and stated that it makes no sense for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lower the exchange rate gap if the economic imbalances the country is facing are not solved.\par \par \uc2 -When the \uc2 IMF\uc2  states that it reached a pragmatic understanding, is it a euphemism because it could not achieve a commitment to serious reforms and that is better than Argentina not paying?\par \par \uc2 -It is the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's dilemma to have Argentina in default, or to move forward with a program that includes commitments from the authorities plus some additional elements resulting from the negotiation, but which are acceptable for the government's balance. The government's conviction that the measures are not the best for the country also weighs.\par \par \uc2 -At the same time, several analysts asked if the \uc2 IMF\uc2  does not endorse with its attitude what many analysts reprove for its "moral hazard" in accepting more flexible conditions to a country than to others, which can claim the same.\par \par \uc2 -There are certainly people in the \uc2 IMF\uc2  who think that it is better not to have a weak program that does not correct the economic imbalances that explain the instability and low growth of the country. And also because they think of the negative impacts generated because other countries may ask for similar conditions. But the moral hazard is greatly exaggerated; it may be that other countries see that, but it is not the generality. I don't think it changes the paradigm of other programs, because other countries hardly want to be labeled as Argentina. The main doubt is whether it is better for Argentina to be outside or inside a program, both for the country and for the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , and clearly with a program a worse scenario is avoided while certain measures are advanced. It is the best contribution that can be made now.\par \par \uc2 -However, observers and analysts, as reflected in the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2 , believe that the \uc2 IMF\uc2  was too lax in announcing the understanding.\par \par \uc2 -I don't agree with that view. The Fund not doing a program is bad for Argentina and for the \uc2 IMF\uc2  in Latin America; the subtleties of not signing it will not be understood and what you will see is a member country that wanted to work with the institution and could not reach the agreement. It is clear, at the same time, that Argentina's problems were not created and will not be solved by the \uc2 IMF\uc2 . At the end of the day, this takes place in a context of political origin due to a distributive conflict that has to do with the size of the State Argentina wants to have and how it finances it. While this dilemma is not resolved, more distorting instruments such as price and foreign trade controls are generated with a short term vision, which in turn generates less investment and growth.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's problems were not created and will not be solved by the \uc2 IMF\uc2 ; the problem is the size of the State that Argentina wants to have and how to finance it.\par \par \uc2 In this sense, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  may have contributed to decisions but it is not the main player. The Financial Times article implies, with an imperialist tone, that the \uc2 IMF\uc2  can change Argentina; if the \uc2 IMF\uc2  wanted to impose a better program, it would have been bad if Argentina did not accept it. The choice was to be a partner, or, otherwise, perhaps to propose a refinancing without targets, so that the country would not fall into arrears. It would be similar to the current negotiation but without the same legal framework as a new program.\par \par \uc2 -Is the influence of the main shareholders on the negotiation of a program real and concrete, as it is usually mentioned? If a US official wants an agreement with Argentina, will it be signed?\par \par \uc2 -The influence of the owners in the discussion of the program is totally exaggerated. As an \uc2 IMF\uc2  official you have a continuous dialogue with member countries, things are discussed. And implicit messages are transmitted because there may be investment interests of those countries. But that does not mean that they get into the details of the monetary or fiscal targets. The same goes for the program with Macri, because the managing director, \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2 , acted as a lightning rod for those pressures. At the end of the day, a decision has to be made whether the country defaults or has a program. The decision to have a sensible and realistic program, which is the best possible one, is in the hands of the managing director and the staff, it is not judged by the shareholders. The discussion with shareholders is about grand strategy. It is also na\u239\'00\'EFve to think that this is a technical body, because the owners are countries, with political and diplomatic interests and that materializes, but in a subtle, medium-term way. And sometimes the pressure tactics of debtor countries are counterproductive. For example, trying to influence a U.S. administration through Congress. I would never have thought of doing that as a Mexican official. Congressmen care little about overcharges, no matter how much they sign a letter to reduce them.\par \par \uc2 The government focused, at least in political terms, on asking for a reduction in overcharges and on asking if it could sign a 20-year program. Was that a good approach?\par \par \uc2 -I think the negotiation strategy was tremendously successful for the Argentine government's preferences: it was like saying you wanted to get to the Moon when you could only get to Chascom\u250\'00\'FAs. If raising overcharges and a longer term was a flag to achieve concessions like the ones it achieved, it was smart and I am surprised that many people did not understand that it achieved its objectives, however poor they were. Of course, that was good on a technical level and not political, because how would we have felt if in Latin America someone from the United States got involved with our legislators? In the United States, lobbying is totally legal, but how would it be seen in Brazil, for example, if someone wanted to influence legislators on issues related to climate change? That doesn't end up helping the new program in tactical terms because the country has to face reviews every three months for several years.\par \par \uc2 -If the program were signed, would Argentina have more inflation and less growth in 2022 than in 2021?\par \par \uc2 -The program will not generate conditions for a sustained reduction in inflation from now to 2 or 3 years. In fact, on the one hand, by reducing the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's financing to the Treasury, this will help inflation to go down, but at the same time relative prices will be realigned, which will generate a higher rise in prices. These two factors in shock will balance in the next 2 years and will be reflected in an inflation more or less similar to the current one, perhaps higher in 2022 and then similar in 2023. But, without a program, the country was going to have lower inflation but that was going to put more pressure on the gap by way of higher monetary financing and that would lead to more inflation. Without a program, the possibility of a scenario of financial decontrol and 3-digit inflation would have been higher. On the other hand, from the growth point of view, sometimes there is a somewhat myopic analysis of the sources of growth in Argentina, when too much emphasis is placed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  restriction as a barrier to growth, since they emphasize the management of short term aggregate demand without explaining how this factor affects precisely the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  restriction and therefore medium term growth. Of course, with the post-pandemic recovery, a low installed capacity and some fiscal stimuli help, but when we return to normal levels of economic activity, the logic of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  restriction is what will determine growth in Argentina. And to the extent that the \uc2 IMF\uc2  program manages to generate some positive net flows with other multilateral organizations and that the net flow of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from exporters improves because there will be greater predictability in the coming months, this will determine that growth will be higher than if there had not been an \uc2 IMF\uc2  program.\par \par \uc2 -What influence do Russia and China have in the IMF Board and how do they usually act in that instance: similar to other powers or autonomously?\par \par \uc2 -In my nine years at the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , Russia was never relevant in discussions on programs in the region; when there was talk of the possibility of a program in Venezuela, we thought about what would happen if Russia got involved, but it never happened. China, on the other hand, is much more influential; in the years I was there, there was a progression of that country to institutionalize its financial relationship with emerging countries. In this sense, it went from a position closely linked to commodities and financial support to the creation of institutions with a broader focus and decisions more aligned with the processes of multilateral organizations, such as the common framework signed two years ago. So China plays a more cooperative role than before, but with a less transparent decision-making system and with some ambiguity. Their strategy is not to kick the board, but they take longer than the rest.\par \par \uc2 -Will the \uc2 IMF\uc2  allow the Government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market to reduce the gap?\par \par \uc2 -It is very difficult to think that the causes of the gap can be solved with a greater \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market, because this phenomenon responds to macroeconomic imbalances. If the gap is not closed because there is no perception that there is greater coherence in economic policy, then \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  is of no use, because at the end of the day \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaves the balance of reserves weaker. This does not mean that in a given period, at a given juncture, it does not make sense to support certain \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the fourth highest inflation in the world and the infinity of controls it has, the diagnosis is that the exchange rate problem is the consequence of deeper problems. And \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  now is counterproductive. It is like thinking that the Argentine problem is the debt; although it is true that the crisis manifests itself in this phenomenon, the debt is the consequence of unresolved distributive problems, which every now and then generate more debt and inflation.\par \par \uc2 CONTINUE READING:\par \par \uc2 The Russia-Ukraine conflict collapsed the world markets while the agreement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  is complicated.\par \par \uc2 The year started with an inflation rate close to 4% and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides whether to raise the interest rate, in the midst of the negotiations with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Three factors contribute to keep the dollar stable in the short term\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220214ei2e006nm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
