\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Argentina's net reserves depleted pending ratification of \uc2 IMF\uc2  agreement\b0\par\par\uc2 836 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Ver\u243\'00\'F3nica Dalto Buenos Aires, Feb 11 (EFE) - The shortage of dollars in Argentina's public coffers has marked the Government's need to close a new program with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ), but the negotiations, which culminated two weeks ago with an agreement in principle that will still have to be approved by Congress, were delayed to the point of depleting the net reserves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The last payment to the \uc2 IMF\uc2  in late January and early February further dwindled reserves and precipitated in the Executive of \uc2 Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\uc2  the announcement on January 28 of an agreement in principle with the agency to restructure 44.5 billion dollars product of the credit contracted during the administration of \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  (2015-2019).\par \par \uc2  "We see at zero the net reserves", said to Efe the chief economist of the Econviews consulting firm, Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Borenstein, who places them at around 200 or 300 million dollars: "That is to say, there are none", so he explained that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must continue selling dollars it will use those of the encajes or will have to achieve some additional loan. Argentina's gross reserves on Thursday were 37,235 million, but the net reserves fall to zero after subtracting the reserve requirements of bank deposits in dollars, the swap (exchange of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) with China and loans from the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2  that cannot be used to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. In this context, \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreed with China to extend the swap by around 3 billion dollars and "to study the possibility that it can be used for other issues than just as an accounting entry" and the associated interest rate, said this Thursday the spokeswoman of the presidency, Gabriela Cerruti. AL DIA The low level of reserves goes hand in hand with the country risk, close to 1,800 basis points, which prevents access to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the debt markets. It also generates devaluation expectations -the exchange gap between the official dollar and the parallel ones exceeds 100 %-, so that, with this uncertainty, exporters wait to liquidate their sales and importers rush them. "Countries keep reserves as a precaution", but "Argentina was not characterized during any government of being able to save", explained Borenstein, after pointing out that a country without reserves "lives from day to day". Argentina reached this low level of dollars despite applying restrictions on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, from registering a trade surplus of US$14.75 billion in 2021 and receiving US$4.355 billion in special drawing rights distributed by the \uc2 IMF\uc2 . DELAY "The biggest deficit Argentina has is one of confidence" because "after carrying out an aggressive debt restructuring" with private creditors in 2020, the country decided, according to Borenstein, not to sign a new loan with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  for "political reasons"; and in the face of that distrust it got "into a kind of snowball": everybody wants dollars, the exchange rate gap widens and fewer dollars are liquidated in the exchange market, adds the expert. Analysts agree that the low level of net reserves does not allow delays in the definitive conclusion of the agreement with the Fund, since the technical document must be formally approved by the \uc2 IMF\uc2  board and approved by the Argentine Congress, in the midst of the differences within the ruling party regarding the convenience of supporting it or not. But if it does not reach March 21 with a closed agreement, the country will have to pay a maturity of the 2018 credit of about 2.8 billion dollars, which it does not have, so it would enter into arrears with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 . "We know that not having an agreement and going into default with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  blows your confidence," Borenstein said. If an agreement is reached, the Minister of Economy, \uc2 Mart\u237\'00\'EDn Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n\uc2 , advanced that the \uc2 IMF\uc2  is asking for an accumulation of reserves of US$5 billion in 2022, something that could be materialized with the principal amounts of the 2018 loan that Argentina already paid last September and December and that the \uc2 IMF\uc2  would return to the country if the new refinancing program is approved. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Miguel Pesce\uc2  stated that he expects a disbursement after signing the program to strengthen reserves. MANAGE SHORTAGE During the countdown to an agreement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  that refinances the loan and avoids defaulting in March, Argentina has to manage the few dollars it has, so economists observe that the authorities have increased the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation and apply greater restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for importers. "Argentina does not have funds for basic things like paying for imports. The traditional recipe is that when you have a shortage of something, the price goes up, so the logical thing to do is to raise the dollar. And if politically you cannot raise the price, you are going to have to generate many restrictions," explained Borenstein. EFE vd/rgm/LLL (video)\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220211ei2b005zu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\