\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Argentina's country risk is among the highest in the world, behind Venezuela, Lebanon, Sri Lanka and Zambia.\b0\par\par\uc2 1,383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Argentina's public finances and, in particular, its external accounts are not improving in line with what a government had promised to reverse "the bad policies" of the preceding one.\par \par \uc2 On the contrary, the reduction of the fiscal deficit in 2021 was achieved at the cost of a generalized deterioration of social spending in order to sustain a policy of "economic subsidies", as defined by the notable increase in subsidies to public utilities, mainly energy and transportation companies, as a counterpart to the freezing of tariffs and deterioration of benefits, as was evidenced in the last week.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, on the external assets side, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily report shows a clear weakening of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, to the point that since November 15 the entity had to sell or pledge (collateral in financial jargon) to reinforce its holdings of dollar bills.\par \par \uc2 The components of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external assets show a clear weakening of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves since mid-November.\par \par \uc2 The combination of these movements, together with the Government's reluctance to present a credible Budget 2022 and to agree to the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's requests to make explicit an economic plan aimed at reversing the huge macroeconomic imbalances, as agreed by other countries, the closest one being Ecuador - September 2021 -, are reflected in the Country Risk index prepared by \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  - EMBI - based on the difference between the yield rates of sovereign bonds (IRR) and those of \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  securities (T-Bonds).\par \par \uc2 This indicator, expressed in basis points, in which one percentage point of IRR gap is equivalent to 100 units, jumped in the first fortnight of January in the case of Argentina by 11.9%, from 1,688 bps to 1,888 bps. The global EMBI index rose by 3.7% in that period; and, on the contrary, Ecuador's fell from 869 bp to 814 bp, or 6.4%. Venezuela also fell by 15.1%, Honduras by 2.2%, Colombia by 0.5% and Guatemala by 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 Various explanations\par \par \uc2 Several factors explain such a gap with neighboring countries, to the point that Argentina consolidates as the second highest in the region in risk perception by international investors, only surpassed by Venezuela with 46,965 basic points, in tune with a hyperinflation and brutal economic depression, as characterized our country in 1989-90 and 2001/02, respectively.\par \par \uc2 In the rest of the world, Lebanon also recorded higher indexes: Lebanon 23,196 bp; Sri Lanka 2,549 bp, Zambia 2,134 bp and Ethiopia 2,003 units.\par \par \uc2 Among the alarms that sounded in recent weeks in the bond market, mainly those listed on international markets, such as Wall Street, the following stand out:\par \par \uc2 1) Mistrust: The movement of the components of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves began to worry investors, particularly investment funds that still held Argentine assets in their portfolios, because not only despite closing 2021 with a trade balance surplus of around USD 15.The entity's foreign assets do not grow, but also since the week after the mid-term legislative elections, gold holdings fell by almost USD 500 million until November 23, then they partially recovered (about USD 250 million), to reinforce liquid foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings, and the realizable placements in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell by more than USD 4,200 million"; and the "Obligations for pass-through \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 " fell by USD 3,866 million, according to the BCRA's weekly balance sheet;\par \par \uc2 To make matters worse, the prolongation of the drought, not only begins to worry the Government, but also investors, because it threatens the contribution of agriculture, especially the soybean-cane complex, to the generation of fiscal resources, through withholdings on exports, and also of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the external accounts of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 2) Negotiations with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 : The delay of the Government in signing an agreement to refinance the bulky maturities of the stand-by credit in 2022 and 2023, by resisting to present an economic program of sustainable growth, with a reduction of the fiscal deficit in a 3-year path, as the organization agreed with the governments of Ecuador in the first days of last September, first before the legislators, which resulted in the rejection of the 2022 Budget, and then ratified in the meeting that Minister \uc2 Martin Guzman\uc2  held with governors of the ruling party where he presented the fiscal path with a seven-year projection, but without defining the guidelines and instruments to achieve it.\par \par \uc2 It is clear that reaching a quick and good agreement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  is a necessary condition to start reordering public and external finances (adjustment in economists' jargon), but it will not be enough to revert the high level of investors' distrust. In 2018, prior to the signing of a stand-by agreement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , the country risk index stood at 465 bp, and in the following days it rose to 479, and continued to rise due to the lack of consistency between objectives and instruments. This led to the extension of the loan to USD 57 billion on September 25 of that year, and once again the EMBI Argentina went from 603 bp to 619 bp. The final result justifies the greater caution that the markets now have in believing in Argentina again.\par \par \uc2 Reaching a quick and good agreement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  is a necessary condition to start reordering public and external finances, but it will not be enough to revert the high country risk.\par \par \uc2 3) Acceleration of inflation: One of the main indicators that denotes the state of health of the economy, because from it derives the socioeconomic situation and the business climate, has shown again in December a violent jump, which reaffirms the expectations of acceleration in 2022 to levels of 55%/60%, as they were not registered until before the Convertibility of 1 to 1, in the early nineties. Although this is good news for investors who accepted the 2005 and 2010 debt swaps, and also in 2020 with CER (Reference Stabilization Coefficient) clause, because it far exceeds the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy reference rate, it constitutes at the same time a threat of default.\par \par \uc2 4) Fiscal Pact: The signing of the Fiscal Consensus by President \uc2 Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\uc2  with the governors, with the exception of CABA, was not good news for investors either, because by enabling the way to increase jurisdictional taxes, it leads to project less possibilities for companies to obtain profits and consequently will contribute to reaffirm local and international expectations of a cooling of the economy;\par \par \uc2 5) Energy crisis: Although this is a phenomenon anticipated by local and international experts, as a consequence of accumulating 17 of the last years with tariff freezes, particularly in the two of the current government where inflation intensified at a rate higher than 50% per year, the blackouts due to deficiencies in generation, transportation and distribution, which are anticipated to be repeated next winter in the case of gas, does not cease to constitute another warning signal for investors, because it makes foresee increasing expenditure items to sustain the subsidy policy; and also of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy gas outside the country.\par \par \uc2 Blackouts due to deficiencies in generation, transportation and distribution are expected to be repeated next winter in the case of gas.\par \par \uc2 It is to be expected that before the next maturity with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , perhaps in the Opening Message of the Legislative Assembly President \uc2 Alberto Fernandez\uc2 , will announce his economic plan, beyond the objectives of form, so that the country risk index starts a downward path, and moves away from the probability that, on the contrary, it will reach the two countries that precede it to share the sad podium of those with the highest financial risk in the world, together with Venezuela and Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 CONTINUE READING:\par \par \uc2 For investment bank \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , a "light" agreement between Argentina and the \uc2 IMF\uc2  would be doomed to fail\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 World Bank\uc2  forecasts that \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growth will fall to 2.6% this year.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  without brakes: the Government is back with new control measures for meat, corn and wheat\par \par \uc2 Absenteeism due to coronavirus puts economic activity in check: industrialists and businesses warn that supplies are in jeopardy\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220115ei1f001sx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
