\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 IMF\uc2 : the chronological and technical errors that refute Macri's version of events\b0\par\par\uc2 1,227 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 By Carlos Burgue\u241\'00\'F1o cburgueno@ambito.com.ar " We used the \uc2 IMF\uc2  money to pay the commercial banks that wanted to leave because they were afraid that Kirchnerism would return...", said \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  over the weekend about the destination of the dollars lent by the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ). And, at the very least, he is not telling the truth.\par \par \uc2 We will only refer here to technical and chronological issues. Let's start by saying that the crisis that led to the call to the international financial body was taken in the first half of 2018, when the Cambiemos government was still in its best political moment. It was coming from a triumph in the 2017 legislative elections, it maintained some political support to advance reforms in the national legislative chambers and its main political referents had wide approval in all surveys conducted by sociologists near and far. However, financial operators saw otherwise. \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 's government could not control the inflationary acceleration, the "green shoots" did not appear and the generation of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the increase in value-added exports did not arrive. To make matters worse, and here there was no one to blame, a severe drought was looming that would further intimidate the arrival of dollars from soybean sales. It was between February and March 2018, after some somewhat unsuccessful attempts by the then Secretary of Bonds Luis Caputo to place debt in the voluntary market, that the investment funds and international banks themselves stopped trusting the government of Cambiemos and began to accelerate the orders of sales of local bonds\par \par \uc2 . The sequence Finally, on April 25, 2018, the day came. At 11:15, an hour 15 minutes after the opening of the markets, to the money desk of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  arrived the order for a double \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , of exit of Lebacs and immediate purchase of dollars, for a very unusual amount: more than u$s800 million that asked to withdraw from the star \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the financial market, in exchange for a quick exit and at a market price of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase, with the aim of selling positions in pesos and leaving the local financial system. The surprise was even greater when the institution's management found out who was interested in leaving the Argentine capital market. The order to sell Lebacs and immediately buy dollars came from \uc2 JP Morgan\par \par \uc2 , the international capital bank closest to \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 's government. If there was a top-tier global financial house that was close and friendly with the government before the world, it was \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 . It had also been a key strategic ally in the first two years of Cambiemos' administration. It opened an office in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-2015, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the most important \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Government of \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 : the placement of the Bonds of the Argentine Republic at the beginning of the Cambiemos administration to pay the holdouts. The good relationship lasted (and intensified) throughout 2017 and until March 2018 \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  recommended its clients in its reports to bet on Argentina . On April 25, the romance ended.\par \par \uc2 And the financial system was shaken. Massive orders to sell positions in Lebac began to arrive from "big hands" -as foreign investment funds that move millions of dollars a day are called in the local money tables- and that, by definition, can decide, for better or worse, the fate of a day. Or, as on that day, the fate of an entire economic plan. The rumor circulating in the international banks that most traded Lebacs (both American and European) was that there were few sellers. No more than three. And that the intention was to get rid of all the holdings in this security and buy dollars quickly, closing the "carry trade" circuit. The price offered at the beginning of the day was $20.50; almost one peso higher than the price of only a day before, so the conviction of the orders created even more anxiety among Argentine traders. The financial chronicle of April 25th described a "perfect storm". All the ghosts that had been lying dormant on the screens of the main traders in the country and the world coincided in a single day: the March rate hike in the United States, the beginning of the trade crisis with China, the local ghost of the indomitable inflation and its battle against interest rates in pesos, the end of the month payments to importers, the delay of the soybean liquidation, the beginning of the application of the financial income tax and its influence on the profits of Lebacs, the eternal debate within the Macrismo for the rise in tariffs, the threat of new rises in fuel and the eventual lack of dollars due to the drought.\par \par \uc2 First signs Analysts were already publicly questioning the effects of that failed press conference on December 28 (2017) in which Marcos Pe\u241\'00\'F1a, live and in real time, took away from the BCRA the responsibility of controlling the monetary base and setting the goals to face the most important problem of the Argentine economy: inflation.\par \par \uc2 Since that post-election day, Pe\u241\'00\'F1a took personal control of the official management of the Argentine economy; a decision that had not yet been digested by the markets, and that generated doubts about the true official conviction of placing the fight against inflation in the top ranking of official priorities. All this in addition to a question that the government still did not answer: how the government would get the more than u$s15,000 million that were still missing to guarantee the payment of the debt for the rest of 2018. A few weeks after that April 25, \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  itself distributed a lapidary report to its VIP clients. In it, he considered whether the tool that the BCRA had implemented to face the run (the interest rate hike) could be insufficient antidote to the evident capital flight\par \par \uc2 that had begun; and he revised for the worse all his forecasts for the Argentine economy in 2018: 2.4% growth and 22% inflation. It also anticipated a dollar at 24 pesos in December. Given what happened at the end of the year, the forecasts were somewhat naive. However, at the beginning of May (when the bank's paper was released), the fact that \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  itself had begun to lower its thumb on the Argentine capital market was seen as a deserting attitude. The truth is that that day began the exchange and financial ordeal that would change, forever, the fate of \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  in power. This was the scenario that led to the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's return to the country. So that the organization then managed by \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  would provide the money necessary for bondholders to gain peace of mind in the knowledge that funds would be available to meet debt maturities. For this purpose, the stand-by dollars were to be used. For the outflows of Lebac positions and other monetary and exchange rate policy instruments, the BCRA's reserves should have been used. Until then, the crisis that led to the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's return to the country was related to the policies of \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 's own government, and not to the threat of the return of Kirchnerism.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020211110ehba007i0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
