\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 20 different dollar rates that coexist today in the local market: the new prices that were added to the list\b0\par\par\uc2 1,585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Every month that passes, exchange controls add a new turn of the screw and, with each one of them, new prices are born in the Argentine market for the same asset: the U.S. dollar. The exchange controls punish exports with a perceived lower exchange rate and reward imports with lower costs than those faced, for example, by savers and investors. It affects digital services such as streaming and forces the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to segment markets to quench the thirst for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of some savers at the expense of others and at the cost of sacrificing reserves.\par \par \uc2 The latest changes to \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  with financial dollars, which are bought and sold in the bond market, created two new prices in the market. The MEP dollar and the regulated or subsidized cash with liquidation with \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. And, in addition, the first one became the reference for foreign tourists visiting the country. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  created a method with which, it hopes, visitors arriving from abroad will be able to perceive a better exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The result of more than two years of capital controls is a multiplicity of dollar prices, some official, others parallel or directly informal. Below is a list of some of the main exchange rates that coexist in Argentina today.\par \par \uc2 !function(e,i,n,s)\{var t="InfogramEmbeds",d=e.getElementsByTagName("script")[0];if(window[t]&&window[t].initialized)window[t].process&&&window[t].process();else if(!e.getElementById(n))\{var o=e.createElement("script");o.async=1,o.id=n,o.src="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://e.infogram.com/js/dist/embed-loader-min.js",d.parentNode.insertBefore(o,d)}}}(document,0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://e.infogram.com/js/dist/embed-loader-min.js",d.parentNode.insertBefore(o,d)\}\}\}(document,0}}}\uc2 , "infogram-async");\par \par \uc2 1) Soybean dollar. The soybean in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a price \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by an export duty of 33% that is applied on the FOB value of the oilseed and that results in $ 66.88 for each dollar exported. One of the lowest, is only the first in a long list of cheap exchange rates that exporters perceive.\par \par \uc2 2) Soy derivatives. The main export products of Argentina are soy derivatives such as flour, oils and residues, whose retentions are 31%, with a dollar received by the exporter of 68.87 pesos.\par \par \uc2 3) Cereal dollar. For other relevant crops, such as wheat and corn, the export duty amounts to 12%, with an exchange rate fixed for these \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 84.62 pesos.\par \par \uc2 4) Beef dollar. A 9% withholding is applied to foreign sales of beef, resulting in an exchange rate of $90.83 per dollar. On the other hand, a lighter retention for pork puts the dollar received by producers at 94.82 pesos.\par \par \uc2 5) Dollar milk (powdered). In the case of dairy, an activity that for some time has been going through a serious crisis throughout its chain, the differential withholding rates are maintained according to the product. They reach 9% on powdered milk exports with an exchange rate for the exporter of 90.83 pesos.\par \par \uc2 6) Wine dollar. In the case of wine, until the last day of 2020 this product paid $3 for each dollar exported and since this year it has been paying a fixed rate of 4.5%, which is equivalent to a dollar of $95.32 for the exporter.\par \par \uc2 !function(e,i,n,s)\{var t="InfogramEmbeds",d=e.getElementsByTagName("script")[0];if(window[t]&&window[t].initialized)window[t].process&&&window[t].process();else if(!e.getElementById(n))\{var o=e.createElement("script");o.async=1,o.id=n,o.src="{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://e.infogram.com/js/dist/embed-loader-min.js",d.parentNode.insertBefore(o,d)}}}(document,0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://e.infogram.com/js/dist/embed-loader-min.js",d.parentNode.insertBefore(o,d)\}\}\}(document,0}}}\uc2 , "infogram-async");\par \par \uc2 7) Dollar peanuts. Exports of confectionary and cracked peanuts are taxed at 4.5% in concept of export duties, which implies an exchange rate of $95.32 for these businesses.\par \par \uc2 8) Dairy dollar. Separately from withholdings for milk powder exports, a rate of 4.5% was set for fluid milk, cheese, whey and butter products per dollar FOB, which leaves a dollar of $ 95.32 pesos.\par \par \uc2 9) Cotton dollar. Currently the export of cotton products and by-products, suffer a retention of income by payment of export duties of 5% on the amount traded, about $ 98.97 per dollar that enters from abroad.\par \par \uc2 10) Rice dollar. This cereal with various industrial processes, always presented in containers of equal or less than two kilos, has aliquots of 5%, 4.5% and 3 percent. That is to say that they receive between 95.32 to 98.97 pesos per dollar marketed.\par \par \uc2 11) Wholesale dollar. This is the one that governs foreign trade \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is traded at 99.81 pesos. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also operates in this market, and it is the price at which it sells reserves when the demand is not enough to be covered by exporters and at which it acquires foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it manages to get the private ones out of the market.\par \par \uc2 12) Retail Dollar. It is, in fact, the price at which the dollar is quoted for individuals. For sale, it is nothing more than a reference from which the taxes that seek to discourage dollarization are applied. But "for purchase" is nothing more nor less than the price that savers receive if they voluntarily wish to sell foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to their banks. It is $105 for sale and $99 for purchase, a serious disincentive to the voluntary supply of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the traditional market.\par \par \uc2 13) Netflix (or Tinder). The PAIS tax on digital services such as Netflix, Spotify and similar is 8% and not 30%, which together with the 35% withholding on account of income tax imposed on such consumption generates its own particular exchange rate of 150.15 pesos.\par \par \uc2 14) "Solidarity" dollar. The retail dollar amounts to $173.25 pesos due to the application of the PAIS tax and the perception of Profits, plus the commission received by banks for intermediation. This dollar value is also applied to purchases with credit cards for goods and services abroad.\par \par \uc2 15) Regulated MEP dollar. If the huge list already seems complicated in itself, here it starts to get a little more difficult. Since October 5th the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the National Securities Commission have segmented the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for bonds. Whoever buys bonds governed by local law - "Bonares"- with the aim of reselling them in exchange for dollars in the local market - MEP dollar or Bolsa dollar- is disqualified for 30 days to trade Global bonds -those governed by foreign law. In exchange, it manages to buy at $179.22 thanks to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling reserves to prevent the price from rising. There is a cap, however, of about USD 15,000 a week that can be bought. This is also the dollar at which foreign tourists are being targeted.\par \par \uc2 16) Regulated cash settled dollar. As in the case of the MEP, the dollar with settlement -which is used to enter or leave the country at a financial exchange rate- implicit in local securities, for example the Bonar 30, has an official subsidy as it is supplied with reserves from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It is at 179.32 pesos.\par \par \uc2 17) Free dollar. The black market is the most widespread price, although it is not relevant in terms of volume due to the small amounts traded. The free dollar or "blue" is offered today at $196 and is one of the most expensive, in this case, because it can be traded outside the radar of official records.\par \par \uc2 18) Crypto Dollar. The purchase and sale of cryptoassets has an added interest in the context of exchange control in Argentina. They are values to which you can convert pesos without a cap on the amount. As in the "contado con liqui", cryptocurrencies are traded in dollars and pesos in the local market; today the parity is about $196.82 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 19) Free MEP dollar. The segmentation between \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  with local securities -Bonds- and those governed by foreign courts -Global- made that when buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with bonds to have them in the local market a new quotation arises, without limits on amounts that can be operated or official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep it low. It is the implicit quotation in the Global 30 and it is at $204.59 per dollar. Who pays more when there is a cheaper MEP, the one that must move amounts greater than USD 15,000 per week.\par \par \uc2 20) SENEBI Dollar or spot with free settlement. This term started to become popular in the last months, due to the new controls of the CNV to dollarize in the Stock Exchange. The SENEBI (Bilateral Negotiation Segment), unlike the PPT, is a parallel round to the "screen dollar", where buyers and sellers agree on a particular price for the transaction, independent of the one set by the market.\par \par \uc2 However, the segmentation between Global bonds and Bonares made the SENEBI become a sort of wholesale free liquidity while the traditional price of the Global 30 now marks the reference of the spot with free liquidation. The highest of all, it trades at 215.69 pesos.\par \par \uc2 CONTINUE READING:\par \par \uc2 Despite the price freeze, inflation was back above 3% in October\par \par \uc2 Elections 2021 and markets: even the possibility of a worse defeat for the government does not excite investors.\par \par \uc2 Ignacio Sarmiento, general manager of Enjoy Punta del Este: "In Uruguay you feel freedom, to move or to do business".\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020211102ehb2003tl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw77\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1155\pichgoal600
