\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Upgrades \uc2 Compania General de Combustibles\uc2  IDR to 'B-'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par 20:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 3 Sep 2021 14:22 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Upgrades \uc2 Compania General de Combustibles\uc2  IDR to 'B-'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -New York-03 September 2021:\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has upgraded Compania General de Combustibles S.A. (CGC) Long-Term Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) and Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (LC) Issuer Default Ratings (IDR) to 'B-' from '\uc2 CCC\uc2 ' and the senior unsecured note due 2021 to 'B-'/'RR4' from 'CCC'/'RR4'. The Rating Outlook is Stable.\par \par \uc2 The upgrade of CGC's ratings reflects the increased production, reserve base, and improved leverage and liquidity profile of the company following its acquisition of Sinopec Argentina. On a pro forma basis, CGC increased its production by 67% to 61,100boed. 1P reserves increased by 50% to 90Mmboe with a 1P reserve life of 4.0 years. Liquidity position increased by USD93.7 million with a proforma cash position of USD244.9 million. The rating case assumes the acquisition was financed mostly through debt with a USD100 million loan from Eurobanco Bank ltd. Fitch estimates CGC's gross leverage, defined as total debt to EBITDA for 2021 will be approximately 1.4x.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 Weak Operating Environment: CGC's ratings reflect regulatory risk given strong government influence in the energy sectors. CGC operates in a highly strategic sector where the government plays a material role; this is further exacerbated by the implementation of capital controls, which has forced Argentine corporates to launch exchanges due to a lack of access to USD and poor financing conditions. Nevertheless, the energy sector is strategically important for the overall economy both as a contributor of economic growth, revenues (through taxes, royalties, and exports), and cost savings, replacing gas imports from Bolivia and LNG, which are more expensive than domestic gas. CGC is a major market participant and is vulnerable to government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Improved Production Profile: CGC's acquisition of Sinopec \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June 2021 increases production by 67% reaching 61,100boed, which bodes well for its credit quality as it add \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  diversification. The acquisition diversifies its production profile by adding production in San Jorge and Neuquina and Cuyo basins, which are prolific basins - the latter of which falls within the prolific Vaca Muerta basin on top of existing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Austral basin. On a pro forma basis, as of end of June 2021, CGC is now the sixth largest gas producer, up from eighth, and fifth largest oil producer, up from 16th, in the country. CGC's 1P reserve base increased by 50% to 90mmboe, but its 1P reserve life remains unchanged at 4.6 years expected for 2021.\par \par \uc2 Strong Leverage Profile: CGC's gross leverage, defined as total debt to EBITDA, is expected to be 1.4x in 2021 and decrease to average 1.3x over the rated horizon, which assumes debt will be rolled over each year through 2024. Total debt to 1P is expected to be USD5.52boe in 2021, after the acquisition, an improvement from USD6.76boe in 2020. CGC has roughly USD119 million of debt maturing in 2021; the largest portion comprises of USD99.2 million outstanding of its November 2021 senior unsecured note that was not exchanged as part of its liability management exercise in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  understands that CGC will be allowed under the capital controls to access the official \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  window managed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Argentina to repay the entire principal, and it reported USD244.9 million of cash and cash equivalents in 2Q21 comfortably covering the maturity. The rating case assumes that CGC will tap local markets to replenish its cash position and will maintain USD500 million of debt on its balance sheet over the rated horizon.\par \par \uc2 Stable Cash Flow Profile: CGC has reported positive FCF of USD25.6 million in 2020 and for both 1Q21 and 2Q21. The rating case estimates FCF will be USD150 million in 2021. Over the rated horizon, CGC is expected to be FCF positive, absent any extraordinary dividends, capex, or acquisitions. CGC cash flows are supported by contracted revenues under Res. 46, which expires in 2021, and Plan Gas 4 (PG4) through 2024. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  estimates the weighted average realized price for gas in 2021 will be USD4.23Mmbtu, and between 2022 through 2024, at least 35% of its total production is contracted under PG4 at a price of USD3.46Mmbtu.\par \par \uc2 Capital Controls Weakens Financial Flexibility: CGC has maintained an adequate liquidity profile. As of 2Q21, CGC reported USD244.9 million of cash on a consolidated basis covering all debt maturities in 2021 of USD119 million and remaining interest expense of USD20.0 million for 2021 by 1.7x. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  understands that CGC will be allowed under the capital control rules to access the official \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  to repay the outstanding USD99.2 million of its November 2021 principal, as it exchanged more than 60% required under the capital rules in 2020. Over the ratings horizon, the Argentine capital control measures that require entities with assets abroad to first use those resources to service international obligations before turning to Argentina's official \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets poses significant risks to corporates in Argentina including CGC, as they will have more of their cash flow deposited in Argentina rather than abroad. Nevertheless, CGC does not face any material maturities until 2025.\par \par \uc2 Derivation Summary\par \par \uc2 Compania General de Combustibles S.A. (CGC, B-/Stable) credit profile compares favorably to Argentine corporates \uc2 Pampa Energia\uc2  (B-/Stable) and \uc2 Capex S.A\uc2  . (CCC+/Stable), and to other small independent oil and gas companies in the region. The ratings of \uc2 GeoPark Limited\uc2  (B+/Stable), SierraCol Energy (B+/Stable), Gran Tierra Energy International Holdings Ltd. (\uc2 GTE\uc2  , CCC+), and \uc2 Frontera Energy\uc2  (B/Stable) are constrained to the 'B' category, given the inherent \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks associated with small scale and low diversification of their oil and gas production profiles.\par \par \uc2 Compared to Argentine peers \uc2 Pampa Energia\uc2  and Capex, CGC's has a conservative leverage profile and strong liquidity that supports its debt maturity profile, that is not expected to be materially impacted by the capital controls, which limits Argentine corporates ability to repay hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt obligations. CGC is an energy company and its business profile compares mostly to Capex and Pampa's upstream business, but both Capex and Pampa are diversified energy companies that generate majority of cash flows from power generation, thus they are more exposed to CAMMESA. Both Pampa and CGC are leaders in Argentina in their respective business \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CGC produces both oil (33%) and gas (67%) exclusively in Argentina, which limits its ratings, its offtaker and impact of capital controls. Nonetheless, its pro forma production size compares favorably to other 'B'-rated oil and gas E&P producers, which will constrain its rating to the 'B' category. These peers include Canacol, Geopark, SierraCol, \uc2 Gran Tierra Energy\uc2  , and \uc2 Frontera Energy\uc2  . Over the rated horizon, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  expects that CGC's production will average 61,000boed, which is higher than all its peers, as SierraCol is expected to produce on average 36,000boed, \uc2 Geopark\uc2  and \uc2 Frontera\uc2  both of which are expected to be 45,000 bbld, GTE at 35,000boed, and Canacol at 45,000boed. CGC's 1P reserve life is expected to be 4.6 years, which is the lowest compared to peers, as Sierra Col's 1P reserve life is 6.3 years, \uc2 Frontera\uc2  at 6.2 years, Geopark at 7.4 years, \uc2 GTE\uc2  at 6.8 years, and Canacol at 6.3 years.\par \par \uc2 CGC's 2021 half-cycle production is expected to be USD20.0boe and full-cycle cost to be USD27.06boe. In line with \uc2 GTE\uc2  at USD20.2boe in 2021 and full-cycle cost to be USD38.4boe, but CGC is higher than SierraCol's half-cycle production cost of $13.70 bbl in 2020 and full-cycle cost was $26.60 bbl. \uc2 Geopark\uc2 , who is the lowest cost producer in the region at $13.60 bbl and $23.40 bbl, and Canacol at $10.72 bbl and $18.43 bbl, but better than \uc2 Frontera\uc2 's at $28.60bbl and $42.20 bbl. CGC's high cost of production is mostly attributed to a higher cost of capital of USD4.8boe, due its operating environment Argentina.\par \par \uc2 CGC has a strong capital structure with gross leverage, defined as total debt to be EBITDA, expected to be 1.9x in 2021 and total debt to 1P estimated to be USD5.52boe. This compares better to \uc2 GTE\uc2  's 3.1x gross leverage and total debt to 1P be USD10.11boe, \uc2 Frontera\uc2  at 2.3x and USD4.98 bbl, and Canacol at 2.2x and USD6.0boed. CGC is in line with SierraCol at a gross leverage that will average 1.0x over the rated horizon and a total debt to 1P of $5.68 bbl, \uc2 Geopark\uc2  gross leverage of 3.3x and 1P of $5.48 bbl.\par \par \uc2 Key Assumptions\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Operations\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 - Oil and gas production to average 61,100 boe/d over the next four years;\par \par \uc2 - Res 46 expires in 2021;\par \par \uc2 - Average realized natural gas price of USD3.50mmBTU flat over the rated horizon under PG4;\par \par \uc2 - Average realized Brent price of USD63bbl in 2021, USD 55bbl in 2022, and USD53bbl long term;\par \par \uc2 - Capex annual average of USD155 million between 2020 and 2024;\par \par \uc2 - Lifting cost (COGS - D&A) of USD12.00boe flat from 2021-2024;\par \par \uc2 - Selling expenses are USD1.10boe flat from 2022-2024;\par \par \uc2 - SG&A expenses are USD 2.00boe flat from 2022-2024;\par \par \uc2 - Exploration expenses are USD 0.20boe flat from 2022-2024;\par \par \uc2 - Other income expenses are USD1.00boe flat from 2024-2024;\par \par \uc2 - Reserve replacement ratio of 105% per annum.\par \par \uc2 Financial:\par \par \uc2 - Fitch Average and EOP ARS/USD exchange rates of $99.58 in 2021 and $142.33 in 2022;\par \par \uc2 - Fitch EOP ARS/USD exchange rates of $115.02 in 2021 and $169.65 in 2022;\par \par \uc2 - Dividends received of USD25 million per annum 2021-2024;\par \par \uc2 - Dividends paid of USD10 million per annum 2021-2024\par \par \uc2 - Full repayment of USD99.2 million November 2021 principal;\par \par \uc2 - Debt outstanding remains flat at USD500 million per annum from 2021 through 2024;\par \par \uc2 - Interest costs are 9% of total debt outstanding.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 3 Sep 2021 14:22 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Upgrades Compania General de -2-\par \par \uc2 --Any further regulatory developments leading to a more independent market that is less reliant on support from the Argentine government could positively affect its collections and cash flow given the company's dependence on plan gas 4 subsidies paid by the government;\par \par \uc2 --Contracted exports with high quality off-takers with a long-term tenure with adequate legal protections to avoid interference from the federal government;\par \par \uc2 --Loosening of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  capital controls rules allowing the company to strengthen its hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cash balance;\par \par \uc2 --Maintaining a gross leverage, total debt to EBITDA profile of 2.0x or less.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 --A downgrade of Argentina's country ceiling ratings;\par \par \uc2 --Amendments to capital control rules which weakens the company's ability to access its capital and refinance debt;\par \par \uc2 --Significant delays in payments that negatively affect working capital, liquidity and leverage;\par \par \uc2 --Revision of existing contracts (PG4);\par \par \uc2 --Significant deterioration of credit metrics of total/EBITDA of 4.5x or more.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Non-Financial Corporate issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of four notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Liquidity and Debt Structure\par \par \uc2 Adequate Liquidity: CGC reported USD244.9 million in cash and cash equivalent in 2Q21. The company has a manageable debt maturity profile with USD119.8 million of debt due by YE 2021; the largest maturity is the USD99.2 million outstanding principal due November 2021, which is the portion of the USD300 million note that was not exchanged in 2020. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  understands that CGC will be allowed under the capital controls to access the official \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  window managed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Argentina to repay the entire principal. The rating case assumes that CGC will tap local markets to replenish its cash position and will maintain USD500 million of debt per annum on its balance sheet over the rated horizon.\par \par \uc2 Issuer Profile\par \par \uc2 CGC is an energy company with \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Argentina, engaged in the development, production and exploration of natural gas, crude oil, LPG (Upstream business) and with a significant interest in a network of pipelines in northern and central Argentina.\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria. ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Compania General de Combustibles S.A.; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Upgrade; B-; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Upgrade; B-; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Upgrade; B-\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Saverio Minervini,\par \par \uc2 Senior Director\par \par \uc2 +1 212 908 0364\par \par \uc2 saverio.minervini@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings, Inc\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Hearst Tower 300 W. 57th Street\par \par \uc2 New York, NY 10019\par \par \uc2 Secondary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Jose Ramon Rio,\par \par \uc2 Associate Director\par \par \uc2 +56 2 2499 3316\par \par \uc2 joseramon.rio@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Committee Chairperson\par \par \uc2 Lucas Aristizabal,\par \par \uc2 Senior Director\par \par \uc2 +1 312 368 3260\par \par \uc2 lucas.aristizabal@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 MEDIA RELATIONS: Elizabeth Fogerty, New York, Tel: +1 212 908 0526, Email: elizabeth.fogerty@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 Additional information is available on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Applicable Model\par \par \uc2 Numbers in parentheses accompanying applicable model(s) contain hyperlinks to criteria providing description of model(s).\par \par \uc2 Corporate Monitoring & Forecasting Model (COMFORT Model), v7.9.0 ( 1 )\par \par \uc2 ADDITIONAL DISCLOSURES\par \par \uc2 Dodd-Frank Rating Information Disclosure Form\par \par \uc2 Solicitation Status\par \par \uc2 Additional Disclosures For Unsolicited Credit Ratings\par \par \uc2 Endorsement Status\par \par \uc2 Endorsement Policy\par \par \uc2 ALL FITCH CREDIT RATINGS ARE SUBJECT TO CERTAIN LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS. PLEASE READ THESE LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS BY FOLLOWING THIS LINK: HTTPS://WWW.FITCHRATINGS.COM/UNDERSTANDINGCREDITRATINGS. IN ADDITION, THE FOLLOWIN