\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Macri unleashed the counterattack that infuriates \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\b0\par\par\uc2 1,057 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Macri and Vidal, at the Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez Blanco Museum It is likely that \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2  has come back from his last trip to Switzerland with renewed energy. At least that's what some of his partners in Juntos por el Cambio say. Days after getting off the plane, he appeared on television in front of Joaqu\u237\'00\'EDn Morales Sol\u225\'00\'E1 with a graph showing the evolution of Argentina's debt. The attack, unexpected and accurate, unleashed the fury of Kirchnerism and reorganized the narrative in the opposition. Macri had been studying. He had a premonition that the taboo subject of Juntos por el Cambio would come up in the conversation, as well as several papers printed in a transparent plastic folder, a tool he uses to lead his own defense. The story of his argument, which opened one of the controversies of the week in Argentina, started inorganically.\par \par \uc2  It all started when Macri saw a tweet from \uc2 Guido Sandleris\uc2  , former president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in his administration, where he warned about the growth of debt in the management of \uc2 Alberto Fernandez\uc2 . The Frente de Todos had done it: Macri could now defend himself with the same weapons with which they attack him. Sandleris's accounts arose, in turn, from the anger caused by a criticism that \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  had made the day before. And they are part of a saga in which Alfonso Prat-Gay and \uc2 Hern\u225\'00\'E1n Lacunza\uc2  also participated. Sandleris spends his time consulting, teaching at the Torcuato Di Tella University and debating with former colleagues from the previous administration. He frequently chats with Macri, who is very interested in the economy after the 2019 setback. A criticism he did not see coming. \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2 , \uc2 Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\uc2  and \uc2 Mart\u237\'00\'EDn Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n\uc2  emphasize that Macri indebted the country in dollars. The former president responded provocatively: if you take the official number, Argentine liabilities grew more with the Frente de Todos than in his administration. A conclusion was drawn between the two after the commotion unleashed by the former president's televised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If this serves to raise awareness that \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to get into debt as long as there is a deficit, they proposed, it served a purpose. The cultural objective, in reality, points to the heart of Kirchnerism's political project. Macri initiated a tectonic shift in his own party. Mar\u237\'00\'EDa Eugenia Vidal and Diego Santilli tour their districts talking about health, security and education. The former governor, however, surprised the policy with a blunt and unexpected statement: \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  and \uc2 Alberto Fernandez\uc2  are worse than the former president right where they criticize him most. It was a prepared move by the former governor. Vidal is a scholar of the issues she talks about. She asks, discusses and questions the technical teams around her before the words come out of her mouth. Her chief economist is \uc2 Hern\u225\'00\'E1n Lacunza\uc2 , first Minister of Economy in her time in the province of Buenos Aires. The exchanges between Vidal, Lacunza and Horacio Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez Larreta yielded four "indisputable" statements that will be the articulated manual to disarm the grenades that come from the other side of the rift: 1) \uc2 Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\uc2  gets into debt faster than \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 . 2) It is a lie that \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  got the country out of debt. 3) The debt is not to finance the flight 4) Today's debt is yesterday's deficit. The PRO's offensive forced the government to get into the details, an uncomfortable space for politics, as Jaime Dur\u225\'00\'E1n Barba taught Federico Sturzenegger every time the latter wanted to give explanations on an issue. The evidence provided by Vidal forced the Minister of Economy, \uc2 Martin Guzman\uc2 , to talk about the details, something that had not happened so far. It can be irritating: \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2 , \uc2 Alberto Fernandez\uc2  and the minister are convinced that the previous government rif\u243\'00\'F3 the country. They said it in public and put it in writing in lawsuits. Now, on top of that, they have to justify it. The most uncomfortable item for Macrismo has to do with the fact that he got into debt in dollars. Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n, on the other hand, prefers to do it in pesos, regardless of how they are adjusted, whose printing capacity in Argentina is infinite. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the debt is "a derivative of second order" for a former minister of Macri. Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n had given some hints of discomfort months ago. It wasn't with words, but with drawings. A graph of the Ministry of Economy presented the evolution of the public debt in an absolutely particular way: while the numbers indicated an increase in his administration, the bars that accompanied them were shown downwards. It is a unique mathematical case: something was rising and falling at the same time, depending on what you were paying attention to. Sandleris and Lacunza are friends, they worked together with Vidal, they exchange ideas and think in the same direction, but they do not do the same all accounts. Perhaps because of his last stint at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the first adds to the patrimonial situation of the monetary body in the red of Fernandez. Lacunza, however, plays simple. He took the spreadsheets that are on the website of Economy to calculate the figure that Vidal reproduced. It is US$30,000 million. It is shown by the government itself. There is more ignominious evidence for \uc2 Cristina Kirchner\uc2  and \uc2 Alberto Fernandez\uc2 : the one who indebts the country is the one who has red numbers. The former president inherited from her husband a positive primary result of 2.8% of GDP in 2007, but destroyed it in seven years. Despite some tricks, she left with a red of 3.8% in 2015. The first year of the Frente de Todos closed with a deficit of 6.5%. Some opposition economists forgive Fernandez for the pandemic slip, but they will not accept that they run with that argument because they point to the presidential formula as a serial indebtedness. The reciprocal indignation moves the pulses in politics and in the country of the deficit, everyone gets into debt. The very logic of Lacunza and the economists of Cambiemos throws another provocative conclusion: the only one who was good with the public accounts in the last time was N\u233\'00\'E9stor Kirchner.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210829eh8t0073k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
